## 1. Introduction
Modern civilization requires energy to function, giving a worldwide energy consumption about 575 quadrillion British thermal units (Btu, 1 Btu is about 1.055 kJ or 0.0003 kilowatt-hours) in 2015.1 As important energy sources and energy carriers, gases (e.g., natural gas and biogas) make a major contribution to the energy production.2 For example, natural gas (main component is methane) accounted for about $29\%$ of the U.S. primary energy production in 2017.3 In terms of energy production, the strongly growing worldwide demand on fossil fuels has brought significant environmental issues, especially the climate change caused by carbon emissions. Compared to liquid petroleum and solid coal, gases fuels are more environmentally friendly given to their lower carbon emissions and higher gravimetric energy densities. For hydrogen $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ and methane $\{\mathrm{CH}_{4}\}$ , their gravimetric heats of combustion are 123 and $55.7\mathrm{MJ}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , respectively, comparing to that of $47.2\mathrm{MJ}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for gasoline.4 The main challenge for gases fuels lies in their transportation, storage, and conversion that usually realize under require harsh conditions and consume massive amounts of energy, owing to their extremely low boiling point, low density, high critical pressure and high diffusivity. The current storage technologies for gas fuel involve liquefaction at low temperature $(-253~^{\mathrm{~o~}}\mathrm{C}$ for liquefied hydrogen) or compression under high pressure (hundreds of atmospheres) at ambient temperature, featuring high facility requirement. To realize cheap, safe and portable storage and transportation, an alternative approach is to employ porous materials for adsorbed storage systems that operate under moderate conditions.
Besides as energy sources, some gases (e.g. olefins gases and aromatic hydrocarbons vapors) are also important feedstocks in chemical industry. For instance, olefins (mainly ethylene, propylene and butadiene) are the cornerstone of many important manufacturing, with a worldwide production exceeding 200 million metric tons, equivalent to about 30 kilograms for each person on the planet. The productions of these important industrial chemical are highly energy-intensive, e.g., the energy consumption in ethylene production is roughly estimated at 26 GJ per ton of ethylene. Prior to the direct use of gas commodities, separation and purification processes are required, which conventionally accomplished by repeated distillation–compression cycling of the mixture. These heat-driven separation processes consume up to 10 times more energy than membrane-based and/or adsorptive separation technologies.5 Given that the current energy use by separation and purification processes is more than $40\%$ of total energy use in chemical industry, tremendous energy savings can be expected by applying advanced adsorptive separation technologies based on porous materials. The key feature of these environmentally friendly, energy efficient separation technologies is the porous adsorbent or porous media (e.g. membrane).
Many common substances, such as charcoal, zeolites and ceramics, have long been used as porous media for adsorbents. To well handle gases, developing porous materials that show large storage capacity and high separation efficiency are urgent,7 especially those with high porosity, and good modularity over different dimensions. In this context, many novel types of porous adsorbents have been developed over the last two decades, namely, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs),8-9 covalent organic frameworks (COFs),10-13 and hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks (HOFs).14 Among different types of framework materials, MOFs are organic-inorganic hybrid solids that are self-assembled through the coordination of organic linkers to metal ions/clusters. The moderate linkage strength $\mathrm{(90-350~kJ/mol}$ , comparing to 1–170 and 300–600 $\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ for those in HOFs and COFs, respectively)14 and vast components variety in MOFs endow them with high crystallinity, exceptional porosity, high modularity and diverse functionality. Thus, MOFs represent the preeminent platform for developing novel multifunctional materials, referring to applications in gas storage and separation,15-19 optical,20-21 electric and magnetic materials,22 chemical sensing,23-27 catalysis,28-38 biomedicine39-40 and so on.41-46
MOFs are well recognized for gas storage and gas separation, owing to their ultrahigh porosity with surface area ranging from 100 to $10,000~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ ,47-48 tunable pore size of 3 to $100\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , high thermal stability (up to $500~\mathrm{{^{o}C)}}$ and even exceptional chemical stability.9 The establishment of permanent porosity for MOFs was realized in late 1990s,49-50 which initiated their applications as adsorbents. The rapidly refreshing records of pore surface area highlight this type of adsorbents very promising for gas processing. The initial efforts to utilize MOFs for fuel gas storage can be dated back to 1997 (for methane).49 Similar adsorptive systems were subsequently applied for hydrogen storage started at 2003 and acetylene storage at 2005.51-52 Since those pioneering attempts on MOFs for fuel gas storage, intensive research endeavors have been devoted to increase corresponding storage capacity, including enhancing binding affinity and optimization over porosity. Significant progresses have been made (Scheme 1),53-56 affording superior performance to those of zeolites and porous carbon. Simultaneously, in MOFs, highly uniform pore size distribution, various functional sites and highly tunable pore size render them the most promising candidates for gas separation. Such potential has been well demonstrated by few selective gas adsorption examples based on single-component sorption isotherms for different gases. Structurally, the development of isoreticular principle57-58 and framework interpenetration59-62 enable the pore structures of MOFs to be controlled at very accurate level,63 while the accessibility of functional sites especially open metal sites64-66 and the implementation of post-synthetic modification67-70 affording approaches to improve selective molecular recognition over different gases. Technically, the utilization of gas chromatography at 2006,71 fixed-bed breakthrough at about 2006-2007 for separating gas mixtures,72-75 and gas sorption site determination52, 76-77 by crystallography technologies dramatically facilitates MOFs in actual separation of gas mixtures. Since then, great efforts have been devoted to the field of MOFs for gas separation (Scheme 2),15-17, 78-82 resulting in lots of progresses especially for the industrially significant hydrocarbon separation.
To date, numerous MOFs have been applied for gas storage and separation. This active field represents one of the most important aspects of energy chemistry and materials. In this review, we focus on current status by selected MOFs examples to provide broad insight for new readers, especially highlight recent advances on addressing challenging storage and separation.
## 2. Porous MOFs for gas storage
MOFs have been foreseen as prospective adsorbents for gas storage right after the discovery of their permanent porosities. Compared with the traditional porous materials, such as activated carbons and zeolites, MOFs are endowed with some advantageous structural features like high porosity, high surface area, tunable pore size and geometry, and functionalizable pore surface. Thus, the emergence of MOFs provides great promise to store some important gases such as hydrogen $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ , methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , and acetylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{2})}$ .83 In this section, we will summarize the recent advances in these three aspects.
## $2.1\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage
With the increase in population and the development in economy, human society has a growing demand for energy resources. Exploitation of fossil fuels has incurred various environmental issues including climate change, which propels people to seek new energy carriers to minimize carbon footprint. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has been regarded as one promising alternative because $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has the highest energy per mass of any fuel and emits zero carbon dioxide. However, the principal challenge lies in the comparatively low volumetric energy density at ambient conditions, especially for onboard vehicular applications. This prompts the development of advanced methods to store $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ efficiently at near ambient conditions.
The U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) has released 2020 target capacities for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in light-duty fuel cell vehicles.9 The system gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacities are required to be $4.5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ and $30~\mathrm{\textg/L}$ , respectively, at an operating temperature of ${\bf-40.60\ ^{\circ}C}$ . While pressure ranges are not given explicitly, operating under a maximum pressure of 100 bar is necessary to reduce the expenses of compression and storage vessel, while maintaining reasonable compacities.
The first example of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in MOFs (MOF-5) was reported by Yaghi et al. in 2003 (although the uptake capacity was corrected until 2007).51, 84 After that, hundreds of MOF materials have been investigated for their potential in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage (Table 1).85-90 Research has shown that the gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and high pressure are mostly proportional to the pore volumes and/or surface areas of MOFs. For instance, NOTT-112, NU-111, and NU-100/PCN-610 are all rht-type MOFs with BET surface areas as 3800, 4930, and $6143~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively. Their total gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ and 70 bar are 10.0, 13.6, and $16.4~\mathrm{wt}\%$ , which follows the same sequence as their surface areas.91-94 The record total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gravimetric uptake was achieved in MOF-210 reported by Furukawa et al. in 2010. With a BET surface area of over $6200~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , MOF-210 exhibits a total gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of 17.6 wt% at 77 K and 80 bar.
For onboard applications of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fuel cell, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage is required to be operated at near ambient temperatures. This task is extraordinarily challenging because the interactions between MOFs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are mainly weak van der Waals interactions. So far, no MOFs reported have met the DOE targets for gravimetric capacity. Though in some cases, the immobilization of open metal sites can enhance the interactions between MOF and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and raise the isosteric heat of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption to about 12 kJ/mol.4 Theoretical studies indicated that the minimum heat of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption is required to be $15\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ in order to realize $\mathrm{\bigh{\Sigma}H_{2}}$ storage capacities at reasonable temperatures.101
Apart from gravimetric capacity, volumetric capacity is another important aspect to consider when evaluating MOFs for $\boxed{\mathbf{H}_{2}}$ storage, because the available volume in light-duty vehicles for a tank containing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbent is very limited and is a critical factor in determining the driving range of a vehicle. In 2017, Ahmed et al. employed empirical correlations and direct atomistic simulations to predict the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacities of 5309 MOFs drawn from databases of known compounds.95 Despite the fact that MOFs with high gravimetric capacities usually have moderate volumetric capacities due to their large pore diameters and concomitant weak ${\bf M O F-H}_{2}$ interactions, these predictions indicated that a simultaneous high gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ densities can be achieved in MOFs. Several promising MOFs were thus synthesized with their usable $\mathbf{\hat{H}}_{2} $ capacities assessed. Among them, IRMOF-20 was experimentally demonstrated to exhibit both high usable volumetric and gravimetric capacities (51.0 g/L and $9.1~\mathrm{wt}\%$ , respectively, at a combined temperature and pressure swing from 77 K and 100 bar to $160\mathrm{K}$ and 5 bar), which represents the value of using computational screening to guide experimental efforts towards MOFs with desired storage performance.
Very recently, Kapelewski et al. evaluated the usable volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacities of Ni2(m-dobdc) $\left(m{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{dobdc}^{4-}=4,6\right.$ -dioxido-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate), which has a high density of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites that can interact with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ strongly. 96 Its adsorption isotherms were measured in the pressure range of 0-100 bar at near-ambient temperatures. Based on adsorption isotherm data, Ni2(m-dobdc) was found to have a usable volumetric capacity between 100 and 5 bar of $11.0~\mathrm{g/L}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $23.0~\mathrm{g/L}$ with a temperature swing from -75 to $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , making it the top-performing physisorptive storage material so far. Its high capacity is attributed to the presence of highly polarizing open ${\bf N i}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites, which lead to strong binding interactions and a dense packing of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ within the MOF. This work may provide some guidance to develop new MOFs with high volumetric capacities for the ultimate purpose of realizing high gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage capacities simultaneously.
## $2.2\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage
Natural gas, whose main component is $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , is an abundant natural resource. The high research octane number (RON) of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and its low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission make natural gas an appealing vehicular fuel. This motivates extensive efforts to study effective methods for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage (Table 2).54 Since the interactions between MOFs and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are moderate, storing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in MOFs can be realized at room temperature and reasonably high pressure, which is practically feasible and holds great promise.
To direct the research of ANG (adsorbed natural gas) technology, DOE in 2012 set the total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage targets to $\overline{{\mathrm{be}700}}\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP)/g or $0.5~\mathrm{g/g}$ gravimetrically and $350\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ volumetrically at room temperature.112 It is worth noting that a $25\%$ adsorbent packing loss is considered. If the packing loss is neglected, the volumetric target corresponds to $263\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , which is still ambitiously high.
## 2.2.1 High gravimetric CH4 capacity
After Kitagawa and Yaghi independently realized the first two examples of MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage,49, 57 considerable progress has been made in this field. Based on the abundant experimental data, it was found that the gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacities of MOFs at high pressure are basically proportional to their pore volumes or surface areas.113 This is straightforward to rationalize because larger porosities indicate more space in MOFs to accommodate larger amounts of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules. Several empirical relationships between the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities of rigid MOFs and their pore volumes have been developed, which provide useful information to guide the discovery of promising MOF candidates for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.
In 2013, He et al. examined the gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities of a series of copper-tetracarboxylate frameworks having similar structures and topologies (NOTT-100, NOTT-101, NOTT-102, NOTT-103, and NOTT-109).114 Their excess gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes were found to rise progressively with the increasing porosities. An empirical equation was even derived through in-depth analysis to predict the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage capacity of a specific MOF: $C_{\mathrm{excess}}=-126.69\times{V_{\mathrm{p}}}^{2}+381.62\times$
$V_{\mathrm{p}}-12.57$ , where $C_{\mathrm{excess}}$ is the excess gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity at $300\mathrm{K}$ and 35 bar in $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\:(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{g}$ , and $V_{\mathfrak{p}}$ is the pore volume of a MOF in $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ . By comparing the experimental and predicted excess gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes of previously reported MOFs with $\mathrm{V_{p}}$ smaller than $1.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , the established empirical equation was found to work reasonably well, which offers a convenient way to screen MOF materials for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.
Six reported MOFs with broad types of structure (HKUST-1, NiMOF-74, PCN-14, UTSA-20, NU-111, and NU-125) were also investigated for their $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage properties using a standardized measurement protocol by Peng et al. in 2013.102 Their results revealed that the total gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake (at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 65 bar), pore volume, and inverse density of MOFs were linearly correlated with the BET surface area. Among the series, NU-111 with a measured BET surface area of $4930~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ has a highest gravimetric uptake of $0.36~\mathrm{g/g}$ . They also estimated that a hypothetical MOF with a surface area of $7500~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and a pore volume of $3.2~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ was able to reach the gravimetric target according to the linear correlation.
In 2016 Li et al. studied the gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities of MOFs at a slightly lower temperature of $270~\mathrm{K}.^{53}$ They found that slightly reducing the storage temperature to $270~\mathrm{K}$ can considerably enhance the total gravimetric capacity at 65 bar. Moreover, an empirical equation $(C_{\mathrm{total}}=-70.463\times{V_{\mathrm{p}}}^{2}+460.543\times V_{\mathrm{p}}-2.709,$ where $C_{\mathrm{total}}$ is the total gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity at $270\ \mathrm{K}$ and 65 bar, and $\boldsymbol{V_{\mathrm{p}}}$ is the pore volume in $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ ) was also developed to predict $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes at $270\mathrm{K}$ .
These established empirical relationships are very useful because they can provide clues about the gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity of a specific MOF simply with information such as its pore volume and surface area. Evidently, MOFs with higher pore volumes and surface areas are more likely to have higher gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities. A viable strategy to improve the gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity in MOFs is to seek or develop MOFs with high porosities. In this regard, Alezi et al. reported a novel aluminum MOF (Al-soc-MOF-1) in 2015, which has an exceptionally high pore volume of $2.3~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and a BET surface area of $5585~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure 1a).105 This MOF thus exhibits the highest total gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of ${\sim}580~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{g}~(0.42~\mathrm{g/g})$ reported so far at 298K and 65 bar, and achieves $83\%$ of the DOE gravimetric target (Figure 1b).
## 2.2.2 High volumetric $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ capacity
Considering that the vehicles have limited space for CH4 storage tanks, a high volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities is even more important than gravimetric storage capacities. The highly porous feature of MOFs showing high gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes usually leads to moderate or low volumetric capacities, because such materials tend to have large pores and weak $\mathrm{{\bfMOF-CH_{4}}}$ interactions. Studies have shown that the ideal MOFs for high volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities should have balanced porosities and framework densities as well as high densities of suitable pore cages for the recognition of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules.113 Accordingly, strategies like optimizing pore spaces and integrating functional sites into MOFs have been developed to target high total and working (deliverable amount between 5 and 65 bar) volumetric capacities.
HKUST-1 is one typical example illustrating the importance of optimal pore sizes for high volumetric $\mathrm{C\hat{H}_{4}}$ capacities. This MOF contains three types of cages with pore diameters as $^{\sim4,\ A0}$ , and 11 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ , respectively, which are very suitable for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.115 HKUST-1 was found to exhibit a remarkably high total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity of $267\mathrm{\cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar by Peng et al in 2013.102 This value meets the volumetric target set by DOE on condition that the packing loss is ignored, and is still the highest reported so far. Wu et al. uncovered that the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption primarily took place at the windows of the small octahedral cages and the open CuII sites.116 Additionally, Hulvey et al. revealed that the small octahedral cages were even preferred over the OMSs as primary $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites, as evidenced by in situ neutron powder diffraction data.117 These results demonstrate the beneficial role of suitable pore spaces in the high volumetric capacity of a MOF.
MAF-38, a MOF with no OMSs developed by Lin et al. in 2016, more evidently reveals the contribution from suitable pore sizes/shapes to the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage in MOFs.118 MAF-38 has two types of nanocages with internal free diameters of ${\sim}6.2$ and $8.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively. These suitable pores give rise to an exceptionally high volumetric total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of $263\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ and a working capacity of $187~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $({\mathrm{STP}})/{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ in MAF-38 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar, which are among the highest values in
MOFs. Computational simulations confirmed that the suitable pore sizes/shapes and organic binding sites in MAF-38 synergistically reinforce the MOF- $\mathrm{.CH_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CH_{4}\mathrm{-}C H_{4}}$ interactions, leading to a dense packing of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules.
Establishing MOFs with suitable pore sizes can also be realized by shrinking the organic linkers of known MOFs whose pore diameters are too large to confine $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules. This is exemplified by Jiang et al. in 2016 who replaced the peripheral phenylene ring of benzene-1,3,5-tribenzoate (BTB) linker in MOF-205 with a shorter double-bond spacer, and constructed a series of new MOFs with acrylate links and different functional groups (MOF-905, $\mathbf{MOF-905-Me}_{2}$ , MOF-905-Naph, and $\mathbf{MOF-905-NO_{2}}$ ).108 With the same topology but smaller cages, the MOF-905 series show $11-21\%$ increase on total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake over MOF-205. Among them, MOF-905 has the highest volumetric working capacity (desorption at 5 bar) of 203 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\:\mathrm{\:(STP)/cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 80 bar, a value rivaling that of HKUST-1 $(200~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3})$ .
Besides developing MOFs with suitable pore sizes, incorporating functional groups/sites into MOFs is another approach to enhancing their volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities. In 2014, Li et al. reported a new MOF UTSA-76 containing pyrimidine functional groups and having exactly the same structure and comparable pore volume with respect to the isoreticular NOTT-101.103 However, the total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar was considerably increased from $237\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ in NOTT-101 to $257\mathrm{~\cm}^{3}\mathrm{~\(STP)/cm}^{3}$ in UTSA-76. Moreover, UTSA-76 achieved a record high volumetric working capacity of ${\displaystyle{\sim}200~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}}$ between 5 bar and 65 bar. Computational studies and neutron scattering measurements revealed that the central ―dynamic‖ pyrimidine groups within UTSA-76 may be responsible for its exceptionally high working capacity, because these pyrimidine groups were believed to adjust their orientations to optimize the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ packing at high pressure. In 2015, Li et al. further expanded the idea and constructed a series of NOTT-101 isostructures containing Lewis basic nitrogen sites, which also exhibited enhanced volumetric total and working capacities at 298 K and 65 bar.119
In 2017 Yan et al. reported that the molecular dynamics within MOFs is a tunable factor to tailor their volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities as well. In this work, three (3,24)-connected MOFs, MFM-112a, MFM-115a, and MFM-132a, with different linker backbone functionalization were synthesized.110 Both MFM-112a and MFM-115a exhibit high total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes of 236 and $256\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 80 bar. More importantly, MFM-115a also displays a remarkably high working capacity of $208\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ between 5 and 80 bar. Based on the solid-state $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy, these three MOFs contain molecular rotors exhibiting motion in fast, medium, and slow regimes, respectively. From in situ neutron powder diffraction data, the primary binding site in MFM-115a was located within the small pocket enclosed by the $[(\mathbf{Cu}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{isophthalate})_{3}]$ window and three phenyl panels. These experimental evidence reveals that the optimal molecular dynamics combined with suitable pore geometry/size accounted for the high volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity in MFM-115a.
To realize high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ working capacities in MOFs, it is essential to maximize the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake at the adsorption pressure of 65 bar in the isotherm and minimize the uptake at the desorption pressure of 5.0 or 5.8 bar. In this regard, Mason et al. in 2015 attempted to employ flexible MOFs to achieve the goal.104 They noticed that flexible MOFs usually exhibit ―gate-opening‖ behavior, which makes a non-porous structure expand to a porous framework as the gas pressure reaches a certain threshold.120 Accordingly, a flexible MOF displays an ―S-shaped‖ or ―stepped‖ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherm. Thus, they proposed that if a responsive MOF can be designed to expand and store a large amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at 35-65 bar, and collapse to push out the adsorbed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules as the pressure is reduced to near 5.8 bar, it would possible to target an extraordinarily high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ working capacity. The flexible MOF they chose was $\mathbf{Co(bdp)}$ $(\mathrm{bdp}^{2-}=1,4$ -benzenedipyrazolate). In situ powder X-ray diffraction experiments confirmed its reversible structural phase transition, and a sharp step was observed at 16 bar in the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherm of $\mathrm{Co}({\mathrm{bdp}})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ with a very low $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorbed amount of $0.2\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at 5.8 bar. Consequently, the working capacities of $\mathrm{Co}({\mathsf{b d p}})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ reach $155\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at 35 bar and $197\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at 65 bar, which are among the highest values at these conditions. In 2016, the same group further systematically controlled the pressure of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -induced framework expansion by ligand functionalization, which provides a tool to optimize the phase-change MOFs for better CH4 storage performance.121
In 2018, Yang et al. report another new flexible MOF, $\mathrm{NiL}_{2}$ $\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\mathbf~{~L~}~}~}=$ 4-(4-pyridyl)-biphenyl-4-carboxylic acid). $\mathrm{\DeltaNiL}_{2}^{}$ is capable to switch between non-porous (closed) and porous (open) phases at a specific $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ pressure.122 The material thus exhibited a total volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake of $189\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar and a working capacity of $149\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (5-65 bar). These examples open another route to develop MOFs targeting high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ volumetric working capacities.
## 2.2.3 Both high gravimetric and volumetric $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ capacity
Highly porous MOFs with large nanospaces are apparently favored for high gravimetric CH4 capacities; however, their large pore diameters would lead to relatively weak $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framework interactions and result in limited volumetric capacities.113 On the other hand, MOFs with small nanospaces and moderate porosities would pack $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules densely through strong $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framework interactions for high volumetric capacities; however, their relatively low pore volumes would restrict the gravimetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptakes. The rationalization suggests the existence of an intrinsic trade-off and mutual compromise between gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities in MOFs. In fact, most MOFs with the highest gravimetric (volumetric) uptakes generally exhibit moderate volumetric (gravimetric) capacities.80 Though developing an ideal MOF with both high gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities is very challenging, some progress has still been made in the past two years.
HKUST-1 exhibits an exceptionally high volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacity of $267~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $({\mathrm{STP}})/{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 65 bar but modest gravimetric capacity due to the limited pore space. To overcome this trade-off, Spanopoulos et al. in 2016 implemented the strategy of reticular chemistry to construct an expanded HKUST-1-like tbo-MOF (Cu-tbo-MOF-5).123 The gravimetric and volumetric BET surface areas of Cu-tbo-MOF-5 were enlarged to $3971\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $2363~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , which are $115\%$ and $47\%$ higher than the prototypical HKUST-1, respectively. As expected, Cu-tbo-MOF-5 was found to exhibit both high total gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities at 298 K and 85 bar, which are $372{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~(STP)/g}}$ and $221\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively. The gravimetric and volumetric working capacities between 5 and 80 bar are also as high as $294~\mathrm{{cm}^{3}\ (S T P)/g}$ $(0.217~\mathrm{g/g)}$ and $175\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively, suggesting the conducive effect of judiciously enhanced surface areas on $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities.
In 2017, Moreau et al. constructed a series of isoreticular octacarboxylate MOFs (MFM-180 to MFM-185) with linkers ranging from 19 to $30\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in dimension for $\mathrm{\overline{{{CH}_{4}}}}$ storage.124 The elongation of the linker in this series allows selective pore extension along one dimension, with BET surface areas systematically increased from 2610 to $4730\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption studies showed that there is no uptake loss in the low-pressure region with increasing linker length, and both gravimetric and volumetric uptakes are enhanced at high pressure. This trend affords an impressively high $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ working capacity of $0.24\ \mathrm{g\/g}$ and $163~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}~(298~\mathrm{K},5-65~\mathrm{bar})$ for activated MFM-185a. The simultaneous high gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities in MFM-185a are mainly ascribed to the selective elongation of the tubular cages with fixed diameter, which not only contributes to the enlarged pore volume but also guarantees the efficient packing of $\mathrm{\overline{{CH_{4}}}}$ molecules within the framework. Similarly, Zhang et al. in 2017 reported the fine-tuning of the pore space and chemistry in a family of isomorphic MOFs based on PCN-14 for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage.107 The resulting NJU-Bai 43 achieves both high volumetric and gravimetric working capacities of $198~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP)/cm3 and $0.221~\mathrm{g/g}$ , respectively, at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and between 5 and 65 bar.
A combined strategy of elongating organic linker as well as introducing functional sites into MOFs was also found effective in increasing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities. In 2018, Wen et al. synthesized a new MOF named UTSA-110a, which was constructed by an extended linker containing a higher density of functional nitrogen sites with respect to UTSA-76 (Figure 2a).106 Consequently, UTSA-110a had a larger surface area and a higher content of functional $\mathbf{N}$ sites $(3241~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $3.94\mathrm{\mmol}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively) than UTSA-76 $(2820~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}}$ and $2.64\mathrm{\mmol}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively). The enhanced porosity may improve the gravimetric total uptake while the high density of functional nitrogen sites may exert a positive influence in the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -framework interactions for optimal volumetric uptake. As expected, UTSA-110a exhibited both high gravimetric $(402\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{g})$ and volumetric $(241\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3})$ total $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 65 bar. With a relatively low $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake at 5.8 bar, UTSA-110a was also able to realize both high gravimetric and volumetric working capacities of $317\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{g}$ and $190\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , respectively, significantly outperforming those of HKUST-1 and UTSA-76 (Figure 2b). Computational studies revealed that the synergistic effect of optimized porosity and favorable binding sites was the main reason for this excellent $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage performance in UTSA-110a.
## $2.3\:\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage
$\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has been widely used for the manufacture of many chemical products and electric materials in modern industry. However, the safe storage of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ still remains challenging due to its very explosiveness when compressed under pressures over 0.2 MPa. Currently, $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in industry is stored in special cylinders filled with acetone and porous materials, which suffers from the acetone pollutant and high expenses. Therefore, developing new methods to safely store $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ is very necessary.
MOFs have been explored as $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage adsorbents.56 Since the first example of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage in MOF $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{2}(\mathrm{pzdc})_{2}(\mathrm{pyz})$ (pzdc $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ pyrazine-2,3-dicarboxylate; $\mathrm{\pyz=}$ pyrazine) was reported by Matsuda et al. in 2005,52 a number of MOFs have been developed as $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage materials (Table 3). Some strategies have been found useful to enhance the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ -framework interactions so as to realize high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacities in MOFs, which include (1) incorporation of open metal sites, (2) optimization of pore space/size, and (3) functionalization of organic linkers to provide specific binding sites for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule, detailed below.
The employment of open metal sites in MOFs for high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake was first reported by Xiang et al. in 2009.125 They examined $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage properties of six MOF materials having different structures and porosities (HKUST-1, MOF-505, MOF-508, MIL-53, MOF-5, and ZIF-8). The results showed that HKUST-1 displays the highest $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ capacity of $201~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm. The strong binding positions are located at open $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites, supported by neutron powder diffraction studies. These open $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites have strong preferred interactions with $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules to result in a high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity. The same strategy was also utilized in the M-MOF-74 series (M = CoII, MnII, MgII, and ZnII) and showed high C2H2 uptakes as well, with Co-MOF-74 having 197 cm3/g at 298 K and 1 bar.127
Introducing organic linkers containing functional sites/groups, such as basic pyridyl sites and amide groups, provides additional binding sites to interact with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules strongly, which is beneficial for $\widehat{\mathrm{\sfhigh~C_2H_{2}}}$ storage capacities.129, 134 In 2013, Rao et al. reported the replacement of the central benzene spacer in the ligand of NOTT-101 with a pyridine ring having functional $\mathbf{N}$ sites, which resulted in an isoreticular NbO-type MOF ZJU- $5.{}^{129}$ ZJU-5 shows an extraordinarily high acetylene uptake of $193~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, higher than that of the prototypical NOTT-101 $(184~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ . Following this work, Wen et al. in 2016 used the pyrazine ring to replace the central benzene to further increase the density of two Lewis basic nitrogen sites in the MOF and generate another isoreticular MOF ZJUT-40.131 Consequently, ZJUT-40 exhibited a further enhanced $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $216~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar) than those of NOTT-101 and ZJU-5. Since these isoreticular MOFs have almost the same structures, the increased $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake in ZJUT-40 should be ascribed to its higher concentrations of Lewis basic nitrogen sites, which might have strong affinity with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule through the $\mathrm{H-C{\equiv}C{\cdot}H{\cdot}{\cdot}N}$ hydrogen bonding.
In addition to pyridyl sites, amide groups have also been found to strengthen the $\mathrm{\overline{{C}}_{2}H_{2}}$ -framework interactions for higher $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacities. In 2017 Moreau et al. designed and synthesized a new copper (II) MOF MFM-188 using a judiciously designed tetra-amide octacarboxylate ligand $5{,}5{,}5{^{\circ}}{,}5{^{\circ}}{^{,}}$ -([1,1’-biphenyl]- $\cdot3,3^{\circ},5,5^{\circ}$ -tetracarbonyl)tetrakis(azanediyl)tetraisophthalic acid.134 MFM-188 has moderate porosity but an extremely high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of 232 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, which represents the highest value observed to date for porous materials. Neutron diffraction and inelastic neutron scattering studies provide cogent evidence of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ -amide binding interactions. Comprehensive investigation revealed that the high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake was attributed to a highly cooperative binding mechanism intrigued by the high density of free amide groups and open metal sites, as well as the appropriate pore geometry. Similarly, in 2016 Zhang et al. reported the construction of a MOF-505 analog with amide functional group in the linker. The resulting NJU-Bai 17 achieved an almost record high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $222.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $296\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar.132 Computational simulations also indicated the existence of H-bonding between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and amide group.
It should be noted that the operating pressure interval for rechargeable $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ delivery is strictly limited owing to its highly explosive nature. Therefore, the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ working capacity of MOF adsorbents is very limited despite having very high total $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity. For practical application, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ working capacity or usable storage capacity has been proposed to be equivalent to the deliverable amount between 1.0–1.5 atm. In 2009, utilizing a unique MOF MAF-2 with kinetically controlled flexibility, Zhang et al. investigated the working capacity of MAF-2 in $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage.135 This MOF exhibits a moderate $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake of $70\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})/\mathrm{g}$ at 298 K and 1 atm, with a remarkable working capacity of 40-fold increase from gas cylinder between $1.0{\-}1.5$ atm. Single-crystal structure of gas-loaded MAF-2 reveals the formation of unprecedented $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ hexamers. Later, the record of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ working capacity was promoted by the same group using a unique metal-organic zeolite MAF-7 that features uncoordinated functional $\mathbf{N}$ sites.137 MAF-7 exhibits a $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ working capacity of $26.8~\mathrm{g/L}$ at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and between 1.0–1.5 atm, corresponding to about 50 times higher than that of a gas cylinder $(0.532~\mathrm{g/L})$ . Recently, based on comprehensive investigation of several isostructural ultramicroporous MOFs with pore size difference of hundredth-nanometer, He et al. discovered that MOF $\mathrm{\overline{{[Cd_{3}(v t z)_{6}]}}}$ shows a record-high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ working capacity of $1.99\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $(51.7~\mathrm{g/L})$ , corresponding to about 98 times of that of a gas cylinder.136
## 3. Pore chemistry in MOFs for gas separation
Besides gas storage, MOFs have also shown great potential to perform gas separations as adsorbents based on their unique pore chemistry. The active field has experienced rapid developments over the past decade.15, 78, 81, 138-141 In this section, we will highlight some of the significant progresses made in recent years concerning several important gas separation scenarios (Table 4), including ethylene/ethane $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6})}$ separation, propylene/propane $(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}})$ separation, acetylene/ethylene $(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}})$ separation, acetylene/carbon dioxide $\left({\bf C}_{2}{\bf H}_{2}/{\bf C O}_{2}\right)$ separation, propyne/propylene $\mathrm{(C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6})}$ separation, $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbon separation, $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ isomer separation, ${\bf C}_{8}$ isomer separation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation, and toxic gas removal.
## 3.1 $\mathbf{C_{2}H}_{4}/\mathbf{C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation
$\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}^{\prime}}$ separation is an important aspect in olefin-paraffin separation, because $\mathrm{\overline{{C_{2}H_{4}}}}$ is a primary raw material in petrochemical industry. Traditional methods largely depend on energy-intensive cryogenic distillation. To separate $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures efficiently, utilizing adsorbents such as MOFs to realize the separation has been perceived as a promising alternative. A number of MOFs have been studied to exhibit excellent separations mainly by one or more of the following mechanisms: (1) equilibrium-based mechanism; (2) kinetic-based mechanism; (3) gate-opening effect; and (4) molecular sieving effect. Depending on whether $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ or ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is captured by adsorbents, MOF materials can be categorized into ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ - or $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ -selective adsorbents, detailed below.
## 3.1.1. $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}$ -selective adsorbent
$\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ -selective adsorbents extract $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures, primarily owing to the stronger framework- $\mathrm{\mathbf{C}_{2}H_{4}}$ interactions and smaller molecular size of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .173
To achieve strong framework- ${\cdot}{\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ interactions, a high density of open metal sites in MOF adsorbents is a favorable feature. In 2012, Bloch et al. examined adsorption ability of light hydrocarbons in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ (dobdc4- $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 2,5-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) and found that the material can achieve $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation.142 As evidenced by the steep rise in the isotherms, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ exhibits a strong affinity towards the unsaturated hydrocarbons, including $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ . Neutron powder diffraction studies indicate that unsaturated hydrocarbons display side-on binding modes, with Fe-C distances in the range of $2.42(2){-}2.60(2)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 3). While the interactions of ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with the metal cations in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ are weaker, as suggested by the elongated Fe-C distance of around $3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity was calculated to be 13-18 for equimolar mixture at $318\mathrm{~K~}$ using ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST). Breakthrough experiments further demonstrate that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ is capable to separate equimolar mixture of $\mathrm{\bfC}_{2}\mathrm{\bfH}_{4}$ and ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ into pure component gases with a purity of $99\%99.5\%$ . This work represents as a good example to illustrate the role of open metal sites $\mathrm{in}{\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/{\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ separation. The same group in 2018 reported the selective adsorption of $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ in $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}(m{\cdot}\mathbf{dobdc})_{2}$ $\left(m{\cdot}\mathrm{dobdc}^{4-}=4{,}6\right.$ -dioxido-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate) also featuring a high density of open metal sites.174
Figure 3. (a) A portion of the solid-state structure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc}){\cdot}2\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ , determined from neutron powder diffraction analysis. Orange, red, grey, and blue spheres represent Fe, O, C, D atoms, respectively. (b) $\mathrm{H_{4}(d o b d c)}$ ligand and the first coordination spheres for Fe center upon dosing $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , and $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ . Gas sorption isotherms for (c) $\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ (d) $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ and ${\bf C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ at $318\mathrm{~K~}$ . Reprinted with permission from Ref. 142. Copyright 2012, the American Association for the Advancement of Science.
In 2014, Yang et al. developed a hydroxyl-functionalized Al-MOF (NOTT-300) with high selectivity for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation.18 The $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption amounts at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar in NOTT-300 were measured to be 4.28 and 0.85 $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , respectively, giving rise to a notable uptake difference of $3.43\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ as well as a very high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity of 48.7. This value is notably higher than that observed in Fe-MOF-74 (13.6), Co-MOF-74 (6), HKUST-1 (4), and PAF- $\mathbf{l}{-}S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ (26.9).175 In situ synchrotron X-ray and neutron powder diffraction experiments reveal that the M-OH groups, aromatic -CH groups, and phenyl rings in NOTT-300 can introduce weak additive, supramolecular interactions with the adsorbed $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ molecules, wherein the CHO distance between ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ molecule and the hydroxyl group is about $4.62\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . However, ${\bf C}_{2}{\bf H}_{6}^{<}$ is aligned at a very long distance to the OH group $(\mathrm{C}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}=5.07\mathrm{~\AA})$ , thus resulting in weaker interactions with the NOTT-300 host. The stronger binding strengths toward $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ over ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ afforded the record $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity.
Precise tuning the pore size of MOFs to separate $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ from ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ based on molecular sieving effect is another powerful strategy for the targeted separation. In 2018, Lin et al. reported an ultramicroporous MOF $\mathrm{[Ca(C_{4}O_{4})(H_{2}O)]}$ (termed as UTSA-280) synthesized from calcium nitrate and squaric acid.143 UTSA-280 possesses rigid one-dimensional channels with the cross-sectional areas of ${\sim}14.4\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ (Figure 4), which falls right between the minimum cross-sectional area of $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ (13.7 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2})$ and ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ $(15.5\mathring{\mathbb{A}}^{2})$ . Thus, the pores of UTSA-280 serve as molecular sieves to impede the transport of ${\bar{\mathbf{C}}}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{6}$ , and allow the passage of $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ , achieving an ideal separation of $\hat{C_{2}}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ mixtures. Breakthrough experiments validates the efficacy of this molecular sieve to separate $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures with high $\mathrm{\bfC}_{2}\mathrm{\bfH}_{4}$ productivity at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 1 bar. Very importantly, UTSA-280 is water-stable and can be readily prepared at the kilogram scale in an environmentally friendly method. These features are important for potential industrial implementation.
Similarly, Bao et al. in 2018 reported a family of gallate-based MOF materials, M-gallate $\mathrm{(M=\Ni,\Phi\overline{{M}}g,\ C o)}$ , featuring three-dimensionally interconnected zigzag channels, whose aperture sizes $(3.47-3.69\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ are highly suitable separate $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ ( $3.28\times$ $4.18\times4.84\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ from ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ $(3.81\times~4.08~\times~4.82~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ ) through a molecular sieving mechanism.144 Particularly, Co-gallate shows a high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ IAST selectivity of 52 with $\mathrm{a}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4\vert}$ uptake of $3.37\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, and is confirmed by direct breakthrough experiments with equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures.
## $3.1.2\:\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}^{}$ selective adsorbents
Typically, ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ is adsorbed preferentially over ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ in MOF materials, because unsaturated $\mathrm{\bfC}_{2}\mathrm{\bfH}_{4}$ often have stronger interactions with the framework due to its $\pi$ -electron density and larger polarity. The desired high-purity ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ products are thus obtained by releasing the captured $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ from the adsorbents, which consumes considerable energy. To find an energy-economic approach, efforts have been devoted to seeking adsorbents that selectively extract ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from the mixture, so that pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ can be produced directly from the outlet of the adsorbent fixed-bed.
In this regard, Liao et al. in 2015 reported the construction of a Zn-azolate MOF $\left[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{batz})\right]$ $\mathbf{\hat{H}}_{2}\mathbf{batz}=$ bis(5-amino-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-yl)methane),denoted as MAF-49, for rarely selective adsorption of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .145 Based on single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis, MAF-49 is a three-dimensional framework with narrow one-dimensional zigzag channels (Figure 5). Since there are only four out of eight nitrogen donors of batz participating in the coordination with $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , the pore surface is rich with electronegative nitrogen atoms. Gas adsorption studies showed that MAF-49 had a higher ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ uptake than $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ under low pressure at $316\mathrm{~K~}$ , leading to an unusual inverse IAST $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity of 9 $316\mathrm{K}$ and 100 bar). Computational simulations indicated that ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecule forms three strong C-HN hydrogen bonds and three weak C-HN electrostatic interactions with MAF-49. By contrast, for ${\bf\mathcal{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecule only two less strong C-HN hydrogen bonds and two very weak $\mathrm{\widehat{C}H\cdots N}$ electrostatic interactions were formed. The different binding strengths and numbers of interaction sites in the cases of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ thus resulted in the selective adsorption of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{4}$ in MAF-49, which was further confirmed by the single-crystal structures of MAF- $49{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and MAF- $49.\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .
Similarly, Hao et al. in 2018 reported a MOF TJT-100 that is capable to selectively adsorb ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{4}$ .176 Based on computational studies, the reversed $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity stem from the formation of multiple C-HO electrostatic interactions between ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and the uncoordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms and coordinated water molecules towards the pore. While the corresponding ${}^{\mathrm{66}}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ -framework‖ interaction is unfavorable.
Apart from hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions, van der Waals interactions can also be employed in the design and fabrication of MOFs for selective adsorption of ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ over ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{4}$ . In 2018, Lin et al. discovered that controlling the pore structures in isoreticular ultramicroporous MOFs to enlarge the weakly polar pore surface of MOFs can strengthen their binding affinities towards ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and lead to higher $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivities.146 They found that compared with the prototypical $\mathrm{{Cu(ina)}}_{2}$ , its smaller-pore isoreticular analogue $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Qc})_{2}$ exhibits an apparently larger $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ uptake ratio of $237\%$ $(60.0/25.3~\mathrm{\cm^{3}/c m^{3}})$ at ambient conditions, remarkably increasing the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity. Neutron powder diffraction studies clearly reveal that the $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{Qc})_{2}$ displays a self-adaptive sorption behavior towards ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , which enables its continuous maintenance of the close van der Waals contacts with ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecules in its optimized pore structure, and thus the preferential binding of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ .
Similarly, Chen et al. in 2018 reported that PCN-250 can be utilized as a ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -selective adsorbent for efficient purification of $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures.147 Configurational-biased grand canonical Monte Carlo simulation reveal that both the pore effect and the van der Waals adsorption energy govern the separation of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ for $\tt P C N-250$ , especially at low pressures.
Introducing peroxo sites into MOFs is another effective method to achieve $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separations. In 2018, Li et al. reported a microporous MOF, $\mathrm{[Fe_{2}(O_{2})(d o b d c)]}$ $(\mathrm{dobdc}^{4}/\Rightarrow2,5-$ dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) with Fe-peroxo sites for the preferential binding of ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ (Figure 6), and thus achieved highly selective separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ .148 The $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ binding affinity was first exhibited in the single-component sorption isotherms of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})(\mathrm{dobdc})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , in which ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ has a much higher adsorption capacity $(74.3\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ than ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ (around 57 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ at 1bar. To structurally elucidate how ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ are adsorbed in the framework to result in ―reversed $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ adsorption‖, high-resolution neutron powder diffraction experiments were conducted on $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{6}}$ -loaded and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ -loaded $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathbf{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) sample. The preferential binding of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ with peroxo sites were found to establish through C-DO hydrogen bonding $(\mathrm{D}^{\ldots}\mathrm{O}\ 2.17\mathrm{-}2.22\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ ), with DO distance much shorter than the sum of van der Waals radii of oxygen $(1.52\textup{\AA})$ and hydrogen $(1.20\textup{\AA})$ . Additionally, the nonplanar $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{6}}$ molecule sterically match better with to the uneven pore surface in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})(\mathrm{dobdc})$ than the planar $\mathrm{C_{2}D_{4}}$ molecule, leading to stronger $\mathrm{~H~}$ bonds with the active site of Fe-peroxo and stronger van der Waals interactions with the ligand surface. $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) exhibits a IAST selectivity of 4.4 for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (50/50) adsorption, which sets a new benchmark. The clean and sharp separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ in the column breakthrough experiments performed with a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (50/50) mixture further demonstrates that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) can readily produce high-purity ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ $(\geq99.99\%)$ ) from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures.
Gate-opening effect can give rise to the preferential adsorption of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{\overline{{\mathbb{C}_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}}}$ mixtures as well. Gücüyener et al. in 2010 reported that ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ can be selectively adsorbed over ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ on ZIF-7.177 This reversed adsorption mainly arises from a gate-opening effect in which specific threshold pressures control the uptake and release of adsorbates. Similar phenomenon was also observed in another flexible MOF, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpee})$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{bpdc}}&{{}=}&{4,4^{\circ}}\end{array}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate, bpee $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 1,2-bipyriylethylene).178
## $3.2~\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ separation
$\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation is a very challenging and industrially important process. Like $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation, one or more of the four mechanisms (equilibrium-based mechanism, kinetic-based mechanism, gate-opening effect, and molecular sieving effect) account for the high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ selectivities achieved in MOFs. Much progress has been made in recent years based on distinct separation mechanisms.
Creating high densities of open metal sites in the pores of MOFs is a powerful general strategy to realize high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ selectivities. In 2012, Bae et al. examined the selective adsorption of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ over ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ in M-MOF-74 materials $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{M}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{0}}$ , Mn, and $\mathbf{Mg}{\dot{}}$ ), which all bear high densities of open metal sites.149 Among the series, Co-MOF-74 exhibits the highest thermodynamic $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ selectivity. Similarly, Bachman et al. in 2017 evaluated the $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation ability of several $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (m-dobdc) $\begin{array}{r l r}{{\bf(M}}&{{}}&{={}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\omega~}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\cal~Mn~}_{\ }}\end{array}$ , Fe, Co, Ni; m-dobdc 4,6-dioxido-1,3-benzenedicarboxylate).19 These materials are structural isomers to the well-known M-MOF-74, but exhibit an increased affinity for $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{over}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}^{\prime}$ relative to their M-MOF-74 isomers, leading to the enhanced selectivities fo $\mathrm{\overline{{\cdot}}C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separations. Among them, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}$ (m-dobdc) displays the highest $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ $(>55)$ selectivity at ambient conditions, with ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption capacity higher than $7\mathrm{mmol/g}$ . Based on in situ single-crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction studies, the notable improvement in ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ affinity mainly results from the enhanced metal- ${\cdot}{\bf{C}}_{3}{\bf{{H}}}_{6}$ interactions induced by increased charge density at the metal site in $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\overline{{m}}\mathrm{-dobde})$ . It should be noted that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}$ (m-dobdc) also displays quite high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity $(>25)$ as well.
While equilibrium-based separations are attained by the preferential equilibrated uptake of one component over the other, kinetic separation are accomplished by the diffusion rate difference of the adsorbates in and out of the adsorbents. In 2009, Li et al. realized the first the kinetic separation of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ on three ZIFs.179 Since then, a number of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation based on this mechanism were reported.
In 2011, Lee et al. synthesized a series of isostructural, noncatenated, zinc-pillared-paddlewheel MOFs from 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(carboxyphenyl)benzene and trans-1,2-dipyridylethene struts.180 Substantial $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ kinetic selectivity was observed in the adsorption. They discovered that the tuning of the pore apertures (via the introduction of Br atoms), the modulation of channel congestion (via the presence of the trimethylsilane group), the thin rectangular shape of the MOF crystals, as well as the fortuitous manner in which the modified channel is oriented perpendicular to the largest faces of the crystals are all responsible for the high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ kinetic selectivities achieved.
$\ln2017$ , Peng et al. reported an excellent performance for the kinetic separation of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ realized in $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{ox})_{0.5}(\mathbf{tr}\mathbf{z})$ at $323~\mathrm{~K~}$ 1 $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}_{0\mathbf{X}}\mathbf{\rho}=\mathbf{\rho}_{}$ oxalate, $\mathrm{~\displaystyle~{~trz~}~}=$ 1,2,4-triazole).181 The considerable difference in diffusivity of $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ results in a kinetic separation factor of 1565, which is primarily ascribed to the appropriate pore apertures. Similarly, ELM-12 reported by Li et al. in 2018 with appropriate channels also achieved the desired kinetic separation performance.182
The size and shape of pore aperture is also tunable by adjusting the topology of the framework for kinetic separation purposes. In 2018, Xue et al. reported the adjustment the rectangular pore aperture size induced by the ftw topology using either the BPTC or ABTC ligand $\mathrm{(H_{4}B P T C~=~}$ biphenyl-3,3’,5,5’-tetracarboxylic acid;
${\mathrm{H}}_{4}{\mathrm{ABTC}}=$ azobenzenetetracarboxylic acid).183 The obtained ftw-MOF-ABTC, thus has the ability to kinetically separate ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ .
Cadiau et al. in 2016 reported the fabrication of a fluorinated porous MOF (NbOFFIVE-1-Ni, denoted as KAUST-7) for the molecular exclusion of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ at ambient conditions.150 Buildt from $\mathrm{{Ni(II)}}$ -pyrazine square-grid layers and ${\mathrm{NbOF}}_{5}{}^{2-}$ pillars, NbOFFIVE-1-Ni is isostructural to SIFSIX-3-Ni. The rational use of ${\mathrm{NbOF}}_{5}{}^{2-}$ rather $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ gives rise to a longer metal-fluorine distance $(1.95\mathrm{\AA})$ and a concomitant tilting of pyrazine molecules. Consequently, NbOFFIVE-1-Ni shows a smaller pore aperture size $(3.0471\mathrm{~\AA~})$ than SIFSIX-3-Ni (4.965 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , Figure 7a). The passing of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ gas molecules suggests the plausible gate size associated with the extra tilting of the pyrazine under ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ gas stimuli. Single-component adsorption measurements indicate that NbOFFIVE-1-Ni adsorbs ${\bf C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ but exclude ${\mathrm{\overline{{C_{3}H_{8}}}}}$ from entering the framework at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and up to 1 bar (Figure 7b). The molecular sieving based $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation was further confirmed by breakthrough experiments with a 50/50 $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture (Figure 7c), resulting in a $\mathrm{C}_{3}\hat{\bf H}_{6}$ productivity of 0.6 $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for a given cycle. The looked-for hindrance in NbOFFIVE-1-Ni dictates the pore size and its maximum opening, and facilitates the selective molecular exclusion of $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ from $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ .
blocks arrangement and its comparison with the parent SIFSIX-3-Ni. (b) The pure $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ (pink), pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ (purple), and equimolar mixture of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}/\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}~50/50$ (orange) isotherms of NbOFFIVE-1-Ni have been collected at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , demonstrating the full ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ sieving ability of this adsorbent at 1 bar. (c) $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C3H_{8}}$ 50/50 mixed-gas experiment using a packed column bed at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and a 1 bar total pressure and $4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{min}$ total flow, confirming the infinite $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation factor. Reprinted with permission from Ref. 150. Copyright 2016, the American Association for the Advancement of Science.
The molecular sieving of ${\bf C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ was also well exemplified by Wang et al. in 2018 with a novel material $\mathrm{Y_{6}(O H)_{8}(a b t c)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{6}(D M A)_{2}}$ (denoted as Y-abtc, abtc $\mathbf{\omega}=3\mathbf{\mathcal{3}}$ ’,5,5-azobenzene-tetracarboxylates; $\mathrm{\DMA=}$ dimethylammonium).151 Y-abtc was synthesized through a topology-guided strategy to replacement of inorganic metal cluster (Figure 8a). The resultant Y-abtc has optimal pore window size $(4.72\mathrm{~\AA~})$ , which falls right between the kinetic diameters of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ $(4.68\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and ${\mathrm{\hat{C}}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ (5.1 Å). This imparts Y-abtc with excellent ability to separate $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}^{-}\mathrm{from}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ based on the molecular sieving mechanism (Figure 8b). Under ambient temperature and pressure, Y-abtc adsorbs ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ with fast kinetics but fully excludes $\mathrm{\overline{{C}}_{3}H_{8}}$ . Multicomponent breakthrough experiments indicated that polymer-grade ${\bf\dot{C}}_{3}{\bf\dot{H}}_{6}$ $(99.5\%)$ can be obtained from a typical mixture concentration of cracking product (Figure 8c). Considering the high thermal and hydrothermal stability of Y-abtc and its complete $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation, this work demonstrates the true potential of $\mathrm{Y}$ -abtc as an alternative sorbent for efficient separation of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixtures.
## 3.3 $\mathbf{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation
Separating $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures is one of the crucial but challenging industrial-scale processes. Current commercial technologies entail partial hydrogenation and solvent extraction, which are highly costly and energy-consuming. Adsorption-based separation with porous materials offer an exceedingly efficient route to performing the targeted separation.
Since the first example for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ separation using MOFs was realized with M’MOF series by Xiang et al. in 2011,152 a large number of MOF materials have been developed for this separation. However, most reported MOFs suffer from a trade-off between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ selectivity. For instance, although the open metal sites in Fe-MOF-74 notably enhance its $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity, the large nanopores lead to a low selectivity of 2.08.20, 142 The same phenomenon was also observed in NOTT-300.112 In contrast, the M’MOFs series have high selectivities up to 24, but very limited $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes owing to their narrow pore space.184
Great efforts have been devoted to dealing with the trade-off in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ separations in recent years. Hu et al. in 2015 synthesized a dual-functionalized MOF (UTSA-100) with narrow windows of $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and suitable cages of $4.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . These structural features enforce its high adsorption selectivity (10.72). Moreover, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups within the framework have preferential interactions with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules to give rise to a moderately high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake. $^{153}\mathrm{~UTSA-}100$ thus achieves a balanced high selectivity and high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake. Hazra et al. in 2017 reported a pillar-layered porous MOF bearing small windows of $5.5\times4.0\mathrm{~}\mathrm{~}\AA^{2}$ . The MOF has a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity of 17 and moderate C2H2 capacity of 3.2 mmol/g at 283 K and 1 bar.154
In 2016, the trade-off was significantly reduced by Cui et al. in a series of SIFSIX materials. They conducted a comprehensive study on the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption properties of SIFSIX-1-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, SIFSIX-3-Cu, SIFSIX-3-Zn, and $\mathrm{SIFSIX}{-}3{-}\mathrm{Ni}.128$ All these SIFSIX materials display apparently higher $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake than $\mathrm{\overline{{C_{2}H_{4}}}}$ . Among them, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i exhibits a remarkably high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $2.1\ \mathrm{\mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.025 bar, with a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity as high as 39.7-44.8 (Figure 9). Density functional theory calculations and neutron powder diffraction studies indicate that each $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule was simultaneously bound by two $\mathrm{~F~}$ atoms from different nets through hydrogen bonding to afford strong interactions between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and the framework, thus leading to the exceptionally high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake and selectivity. Column breakthrough experiments performed with 1/99 and $50/50~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ mixtures further confirm the excellent separation ability of SIFSIX-2-Cu-i.
To fully address the issue of trade-off in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ separation, Li et al. in 2018 constructed an ideal $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecular sieve SIFSIX-14-Cu-i (also termed as UTSA-200) using a shorter organic linker of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -azopyridine $(9.0\mathrm{~\normalfont~\AA~})$ instead of $^{4,4^{\dag}}$ -dipyridylacetylene $(9.6\mathrm{\AA})$ in SIFSIX-2-Cu-i.155 UTSA-200 is isoreticular to the net of SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, but possesses contracted pore size (3.4 Å). Structural and modeling studies revealed that this smaller pore size in UTSA-200 can completely block the entrance of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecules (Figure 10a). Consequently, UTSA-200a exhibits a steep and high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $116\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and 1 bar in pure component adsorption isotherms (Figure 10b). By contrast, the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ uptake is about merely $0.25\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and at a pressure up to 0.7 bar, which is dramatically lower than that of SIFSIX-2-Cu-i. Based on neutron powder diffraction studies, the ultrastrong $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in UTSA-200 mainly originates from the stronger $\mathrm{C\equivD\cdotsF}$ hydrogen bonding (1.921 Å) compared to SIFSIX-2-Cu-i (2.015 Å). Due to the molecular exclusion of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ultrastrong $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capture ability, UTSA-200a exhibits the record selectivity of over 6000 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar for $1/99\ C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm C_{2}\mathrm H_{4}$ mixture, and also displays the highest $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption amount $\left(1.74\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right)$ from the gas mixture of this component. In breakthrough experiments, UTSA-200a achieves a record purification capacity for removal of trace $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ with a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $1.18\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ from $1/99\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ mixture to produce ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ with a purity of $99.9999\%$ (Figure 10c). UTSA-200a thus successfully overcomes the trade-off in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ separation and sets up new benchmarks in terms of both the capacity and selectivity.
## 3. $\mathbf{4}~\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ separation
$\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , as a critical feedstock chemical and fuel gas, is generally produced by partial combustion of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ or cracking of hydrocarbons. Since $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ commonly exists in industrial processes, it is important to remove $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ mixtures to generate $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ with high-purity. Compared with the traditional method of cryogenic distillation or solvent extraction, separation realized by selective adsorption of one species over the other is eco-friendly and energy-economic. However, exploration of such adsorbents remains a great challenge, owing to their very similar physical properties like molecular size $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $3.32\times3.34\times5.70\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ ; $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $3.18\times3.33\times5.36$ $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3\setminus}$ ) and boiling point $(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $-84~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $-78.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ ). Nevertheless, some MOFs still realized the desired $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation in recent years.
The immobilization of functional sites in MOFs has been well documented as a promising strategy to enhance the recognition ability of MOFs for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . In 2016, Luo et al. reported $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(H_{2}O)(d o b d c){\cdot}0.5(H_{2}O)}$ (termed as UTSA-74), $\vec{\textbf{a}}$ new isomeric Zn-MOF-74, for a highly selective $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation.156 Unlike the metal sites in Zn-MOF-74 that only bear the five-coordinate square pyramidal coordination geometry, the ones in UTSA-74 have two categories. Within the binuclear secondary building units in UTSA-74, one $Z\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{II}}\left(Z\mathrm{n}1\right)$ is a tetrahedral while the other $\mathbf{\chi}(Z\mathbf{n}2)$ is an octahedral coordination geometry. After activation, the two axial water molecules on $Z\mathbf{n}2$ sites can be removed to produce two binding sites per Zn2 ion. Accordingly, UTSA-74 adsorbs comparably amount of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ $(145\mathrm{~\cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3})$ with respect to Zn-MOF-74. In terms of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, $Z\mathrm{n}2$ sites in UTSA-74 are bridged by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, as opposed to being terminally bound in Zn-MOF-74. This feature leads to its much lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake $(90~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3})$ and a high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity of 9 at 100 kPa and $298\mathrm{K}$ .
In 2017, Lin et al. discovered a novel microporous SIFSIX-MOF, $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{dps})_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})]$ 1 $\mathrm{(dps=4,4^{\circ}}$ -dipyridylsulfide; termed as $\mathrm{UTSA}–300)$ for the optimized separation of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ .157 UTSA-300 possesses two-dimensional channels of ${\sim}3.3$ Å (Figure 11), which transforms into its closed-pore phase after activation/desolvation. The shrinked structure is locked by strong C-HF hydrogen bonds and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions. Interestingly, UTSA-300a adsorbs $76.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ but negligible amounts of $\overrightarrow{\mathrm{{CO}_{2}^{\ast}}}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ under ambient conditions. High-resolution neutron powder diffraction studies and molecular modeling reveal that this phenomenon is attributed to the breaking of the original intranetwork hydrogen bond by $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules. Specifically, one $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules primarily bind to two hexafluorosilicate F atoms in a head-on orientation, and consequently expand the framework to its pore-open structure. Such pore-opening behavior was not observed in the cases of $\mathrm{\bar{CO}}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ due to the different binding modes and unfavorable pore size. Breakthrough Experiments further confirm the excellent separation ability of UTSA-300a for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures.
Figure 11. (a) Perspective views of cage unit in UTSA-300 showing aperture size of $3.3\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ (Zn, Si, F, S, N, and C are represented by purple, orange, green, bright yellow, light blue, and gray, respectively, and solvent molecules are omitted for clarity). (b) $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ single-component sorption isotherms for UTSA-300a at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ . (c) Comparison of uptakes ratio among UTSA-300a and representative MOFs at ambient conditions. (d) Preferential binding sites in UTSA-300 for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ molecules shown with Hirshfeld surface $(d_{\mathrm{i}})$ displaying C-DF interactions (red area). (e) Packing diagram of UTSA- $300{\scriptstyle\=\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}}$ from neutron powder diffraction data. $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ molecules are shown in a CPK model. (f) Experimental column breakthrough curves for equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ (purple) and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (orange) mixtures (298 K, 1 bar) in an adsorber bed packed with UTSA-300a. Reprinted with permission from Ref. 157. Copyright 2017, American Chemical Society.
While most MOFs preferentially adsorb $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is produced from the release of the captured $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which inevitably consumes considerable energy. In an ideal $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ separation scenario, adsorbents are required to selectively adsorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , so that pure $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ can be generated directly during the ―adsorption‖ process. In 2016, Foo et al. reported a new flexible MOF $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{dpe})]$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}}&{{}\ =\ }&{1,4\cdot}\end{array}$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid, dpe $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 1,2-di(4-pyridyl)ethylene) with preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .159 Interestingly, this MOF has a moderate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $48~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar with no gate-opening effect. By contrast, a gate-opening effect was observed in the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ with a steep step occurring at over 1 bar. The low $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake at 1 bar $(10~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ suggests that this MOF is capable to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , as confirmed by simulated and experimental breakthrough experiments.
In 2016, Chen et al. reported two structurally close-related MOFs (SIFSIX-3-Ni and TIFSIX-2-Cu-i) that display distinct preferential adsorption behaviors of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ mixtures.158 SIFSIX-3-Ni is a non-interpenetrated MOF with pore size of ${\sim}4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , while TIFSIX-2-Cu-i is a double-interpenetrated framework with a slightly large pore size of ${\sim}5.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Gas sorption analysis indicates that TIFSIX-2-Cu-i exhibits a preferential adsorption of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , while SIFSIX-3-Ni has higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ below 0.3 bar at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . Such distinct adsorption behaviors are associated with the different pore sizes of the frameworks and the concomitant different binding sites, which are evidenced by molecular simulation experiments. Specifically, the pores in SIFSIX-3-Ni match well with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules to result in strong interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ sites, whereas TIFSIX-2-Cu-i has more pore space to trap the slightly longer $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules and thus exhibits preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
## $3.5\:\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{6}$ separation
${\bf C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ is one prime raw materials for petrochemical production, and is primarily produced by steam cracking. The raw $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ product is unavoidably impurified by a trace amount of $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ . To meet the criterion of polymer-grade ${\dot{\mathrm{C}}}_{3}{\dot{\mathrm{H}}}_{6},$ , the $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ concentration is required to be less than 5 ppm. Compared with traditional cryogenic distillation, adsorptive separation of $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ from ${\bf C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ with porous materials is deemed as a more economic and energy-efficient method. However, the removal of trace $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ (typically $1\%$ ) from ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is very challenging because of their similar molecular sizes $(\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{4}6.2\times3.8\times3.8\stackrel{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ ; $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}},6.5\times4.0\times4.2\mathrm{\stackrel{\circ}{A}}^{3})$ and other physical properties. So far, only several MOFs have been developed and show potential in this important but challenging separation.
In 2017, Li et al. reported the first example of using a flexible-robust MOF, $\mathrm{[Cu(bpy)_{2}(O T f)_{2}]}$ (ELM-12, bpy $=4\mathrm{,}4\mathrm{^{\circ}}$ -bipyridine, OTf- $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ trifluoromethanesulfonate), to separate $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ from $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixture (1/99, v/v).160. ELM-12 is composed of a rigid square-grid copper bipyridine scaffold with dangling OTf groups. It has two types of cavities bearing a dumbbell-shape (Type I, pocket size $6.1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times4.3\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times4.3\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ and an ellipsoid-shaped ( $\mathrm{Type\II,6.8\\mathring{A}\times4.0\ \mathring{A}\times4.2\ \mathring{A})}$ , respectively (Figure 12). These cavities match evidently better with the size and shape of $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ $(6.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\times3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ $\times3.8\mathrm{\AA}$ than those of $\mathrm{\hat{C}_{3}H_{6}}$ $(6.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\times4.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\times3.8\mathrm{~\AA~})$ , consequently ELM-12 exhibits notably a higher $\mathrm{\overline{{C}}}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptake $\mathrm{(1.83\mmol/g)}$ than that of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ $(0.67\mathrm{\mmol/g})$ in gas sorption isotherms at 298 K and 0.01 bar, with adsorption selectivity reaching up to 84 for 1/99 mixture. Breakthrough experiments revealed that ELM-12 can effectively remove trace $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ from 1/99 $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixtures at ambient conditions to produce ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ with a purity of $99.9998\%$ . High-resolution neutron powder diffraction data indicated the existence of two $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ binding sites in ELM-12, in both of which $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ molecules interact with OTf- groups through C-DO hydrogen bonds. The strong binding affinity and suitable pore confinement for $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ in ELM-12 account for its high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ separation ability.
In 2018, Yang et al. investigated $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ separation performance of a series of SIFSIX MOFs (SIFSIX-1-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, and SIFSIX-3-Ni).161 All three SIFSIX materials displayed steep $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ uptakes in the low-pressure region of the adsorption isotherms at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , and larger adsorption amounts of $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ than $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ at 1 bar. In particular, the pyrazine based ultramicroporous SIFSIX-3-Ni has a near-saturation $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ uptake of $2.65\mathrm{mmol/g}$ at an extremely low pressure (0.003 bar) with $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ selectivity over 250, suggesting the existence of strong $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ interaction sites within SIFSIX-3-Ni. Based on the first-principle calculations and X-ray diffraction experiments, the pore space $(7.5\times4.6\times4.6\mathrm{~\normalfont~\AA~}^{3})$ and the geometric disposition of $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ anions in SIFSIX-3-Ni match well with the $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ molecule in terms of both molecular shape and hydrogen bonding sites, enabling the efficient single molecular trap of $\mathrm{\hat{C}}_{3}\mathrm{H}4$ for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ separation. Breakthrough experiments further confirm that SIFSIX-3-Ni can remove trace $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ from $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ with a purification capacity of $\mathrm{1187_{mL/g}}$ at ultralow concentration of $1000\mathrm{ppm}$ .
In 2018, Yang et al. further reported the one-step purification of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from multicomponent gases using a single material NbOFFIVE-2-Cu-i (also termed as ZU-62).162 ZU-62 features asymmetric $\mathrm{O/F}$ node coordination, which leads to the formation of three types of nanopores bearing aperture sizes of $6.75\mathrm{\AA}$ (Site I), $6.94\mathrm{\AA}$ (Site II), $7.20\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Site III), respectively. These multiple binding sites enabled the simultaneously preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $\left(1.87\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right)$ and propadiene (1.74 $\mathrm{mmol/g)}$ over ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ in gas sorption isotherms at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and ultra-low pressure (5000 ppm ) in ZU-62. The multisite capture mechanism was revealed by dispersion-corrected density functional theory calculations. This work provides some clues for the one-step purification from multicomponent mixtures.
In 2018, Li et al. conducted a comprehensive screening of a series of MOFs with broad types of structures, pore sizes, and functionalities (Figure 13a).185 Among them, the ultramicropous UTSA-200 $\mathrm{([Cu(azpy)_{2}(S i F_{6})]_{n}}$ , $\mathsf{a z p y}=4{,}4^{,}$ -azopyridine) was identified as the best-performing material to remove trace amount of $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ from $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixtures. UTSA-200 exhibits by far the highest $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ uptake $(95~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at 0.01 bar and $298\ \mathrm{K})$ ) and record high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ selectivity (over 20000) based on gas sorption isotherms (Figure 13b), which set new benchmarks among all types of materials. Breakthrough experiments revealed that UTSA-200 can completely remove trace $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ from 1:99 and 0.1:99.9 (v/v) mixtures, thus producing $99.9999\%$ pure ${\bf C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ with productivities of 62.0 and $142.8~\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ , respectively. The strong $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ separation ability mainly originates from the suitable dynamic pore size of UTSA-200 to block the passage of larger $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ molecules, as well as the strong binding sites and pore flexibility associated with the rotation of pyridine rings to effectively capture smaller-sized $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{4}}}$ .
## $3.6\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbon separation
$\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbon separation is an important but challenging process in hydrocarbon purifications, because 1,3-butadiene $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6})}$ , 1-butene $(n{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8})$ , and isobutene $(i s o\mathrm{-C_{4}H_{8}})$ are essential raw materials for the production of a variety of synthetic rubbers and chemicals. However, their very similar structures bring considerable difficulty in efficient separation. Nevertheless, some MOFs still have been fabricated to address this challenging task.
In 2015, Assen et al. synthesized a novel rare-earth metal (RE) fcu-MOF with a suitable aperture size to achieve the practical steric adsorptive separation of branched paraffins from normal ones.163 They judiciously chose a short linker fumarate (fum) to construct RE-fum-fcu-MOF with a contracted triangular aperture size of about $4.7\mathring\mathrm{\textrm{A}}$ . Because the optimal aperture is the sole access to the framework cages, they function as sorbate-size cutoffs, enabling a complete molecular sieving of branched paraffins from normal ones. Consequently, RE-fum-fcu-MOF displays a fully reversible Type-I gas sorption isotherm for $\scriptstyle n-\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathbf{H}_{10}$ at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ , while no iso- $\mathbf{\cdotC_{4}H_{10}}$ is adsorbed. The $\boldsymbol{n}{-}\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathbf{H}_{10}/i s o{-}\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathbf{H}_{10}$ separation ability in RE-fum-fcu-MOF is further confirmed by breakthrough experiments using $n{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}/i s o{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\colon\pm$ 5/5/90 gas mixture performed at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. As expected, iso- ${\cdot}{\bf C}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{10}$ is not retained in the column, while $\scriptstyle n-\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathbf{H}_{10}$ is retained in the column for about $17~\mathrm{min}$ with an adsorbed amount of 0.8 $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ .
In 2017, Zhang et al. reported a series of SIFSIX materials (GeFSIX-2-Cu-i (ZU-32), NbFSIX-2-Cu-i (ZU-52), GeFSIX-14-Cu-i (ZU-33)), which exhibit selective recognition of specific $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins.164 They successfully fine-tuned the cavity size and functional site disposition with increments in $\mathrm{0.2\textup{\AA}}$ scale in the MOFs by rationally altering the anion pillars and organic linkers. GeFSIX-14-Cu-i, in particular, whose pores can have a size of $4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , if the plausible complete omission of the occurring steric hindrance of azpy (azpy $\mathbf{\Psi}=4,4\mathbf{\Psi}$ -azopyridine) linker is considered. This pore is larger than the smallest cross sections of $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{4}H_{6}}$ and narrower than those of $n{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ and iso- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , which makes the size exclusion of specific $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins in GeFSIX-14-Cu-i feasible. With the combined effect of molecular recognition via hydrogen bonding and size-sieving, these materials unexpectedly achieve the sieving separation of $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}/n\mathrm{-C_{4}H_{8}}$ , C4H6/iso- ${\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{4}\mathrm{{H}}_{8}$ , and $\boldsymbol{n}{-}\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8}/\mathrm{i}\mathbf{s}_{0}{-}\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ with high capacities, as confirmed by breakthrough experiments.
MOFs with functional sites/groups commonly adsorb $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ preferentially over other $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbons, because of its high degree of unsaturation and strong coordination ability. $\mathrm{\overline{{C_{4}H_{6}}}}$ is thus purified in a two-step process consisting of $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ capture and its subsequent release, which is energy-intensive and might induce undesired polymerization as well. To deal with this drawback, Liao et al. in 2017 fabricated a hydrophilic MOF $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{btm})_{2}]$ also termed as Zn-BTM, $\mathrm{(H_{2}b t m~=~}$ bis(5-methyl- $1H^{\chi}1,2,4$ -triazol-3-yl)methane)) featuring flexible quasi-discrete pores and achieved the reversed preferential adsorption of $n{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , iso- $\mathrm{\cdotC_{4}H_{8}}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{4}H_{10}}}$ over $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ with the MOF (Figure 14a).186 Zn-BTM display the $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbon adsorption slopes in the sequence of $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}<\mathrm{C_{4}H_{10}}<n\mathrm{-C_{4}H_{8}}<i\mathrm{-C_{4}H_{8}}$ at low pressure region in their single-component isotherms (Figure 14b), which clearly indicates the preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{{C_{4}H_{10}}}$ , $\scriptstyle n-\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathbf{H}_{8}$ , and $i{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ over $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ . Based on single crystal X-ray diffraction studies and computational simulations, the preferential adsorption derives from the conformational changes induced in the flexible $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ molecules by quasi-discrete pores in Zn-BTM (Figure 14c). While such a conformational change is difficult to occur in $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ due to a large bending energy penalty. In breakthrough experiments with $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTM, $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ eluted first, followed by $\mathrm{{C_{4}H_{10}}}$ , $n{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , and $i{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , further demonstrating that Zn-BTM can efficiently purify $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ with a desired purity $(\geq99.5\%)$ .
## 3.7 Separation of $\bf{C_{6}}$ isomers
Separating hexane isomers is regarded as a crucial process involved in petroleum industry, because it is closely related to the enhancement of octane ratings in gasoline. Hexane isomers include unreacted n-hexane (nHEX), monobranched isomers 2-methylpentane (2MP), 3-methylpentane (3MP), dibranched isomers 2,2-dimethylbutane (22DMB) and 2,3-dimethylbutane (23DMB). At certain temperatures in ambient conditions, $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ isomers exist in the form of gas vapor. In this scenario, employing MOFs to perform adsorption-based separation of these $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ isomers are favorable.
The first example of using microporous MOF to separate alkane isomers was reported by Chen et al. in 2006.71 They successfully separated $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ hydrocarbons by means of gas chromatography with MOF-508 as the column filler. In the same year, Pan et al. realized the separation of $\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathbf{-C}_{6}$ hydrocarbons using a Cu-based microporous MOF.187After that, Bárcia et al. achieved a kinetic separation of hexane isomers using $\mathrm{Zn(bdc)}(\mathrm{dabco)}_{0.5}$ $(\mathrm{bdc}^{2-}=1,4$ -benzenedicarboxylate; dabco $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) by fixed-bed adsorption.73 Recently, a number of interesting works have been reported successively.
In 2013 Herm et al. reported utilizing a framework $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}$ $({\bf B}{\bf D}{\bf P}^{2-}{\bf\Phi}={\bf\Phi}$ 1,4-benzenedipyrazolate) featuring triangular channels to separate hexane isomers (Figure 15).188 Single-component adsorption isotherms and isosteric heat of adsorption of these isomers suggest that the higher degree of branching would lead to stronger adsorption strength. In breakthrough experiments conducted with a equimolar mixture of nHEX, 2MP, 3MP, 23DMB, and 22DMB, dibranched isomers eluted first, followed by monobranched isomer, and finally the linear isomer. Configurational-bias Monte Carlo simulations indicates that the origin of separation lies in the fact that the number of carbon atoms that can interact with the pore surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}$ reduces with the increasing degree of branching. Thus, DMB isomers have the weakest interactions with MOF surface and wedge along the triangular channels most quickly.
In 2018, Wang et al. for the first time achieved a temperature- and adsorbate-dependent molecular sieving of linear, monobranched, and dibranched hexane isomers using a calcium-based MOF $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ (tcpb $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)-benzene) that exhibits.189 $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ possesses one-dimensional channels bearing a size of $5.5{-6\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ , which is close to the kinetic diameters of branched hexane isomers $(5.0{-}6.2\mathrm{~\AA})$ . By screening the adsorption amounts of nHEX, 3MP, and 22DMB at various temperatures in $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ , they found that the MOF is able to adsorb nHEX but exclude 3MP and 22DMB at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Interestingly at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ takes up nHEX and 3MP but blocks 22DMB. Taking advantage of the adsorption properties of $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ , a successful separation of the ternary mixture into pure components was thus achieved through a two-column ( $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ respectively) breakthrough system. The same group further reported a topologically guided synthesis of a series of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs with rationally tuned pore structures for $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ isomer separation. Among the series, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{bptc})_{3}$ (bptc $=3{,}3{^{\circ}},5{,}5{^{\circ}}$ -biphenyltetracarboxylate) adsorbs a large amount of nHEX but excludes branched isomers. While $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{8}(-H_{2}O)_{4}(a b t c)_{2}}$ (abtc4- $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -azobenzene-tetracarboxylate) is able to discriminate all three $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ isomers with a high 3MP/22DMB separation factor.190
## ${\bf3.8C O}_{2}$ capture and separation
The atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration has risen sharply due to anthropogenic activities, with the primary emission coming from the combustion of fossil fuels. Considerable research efforts have been devoted to developing MOFs as adsorbents for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.191 Some thematic reviews have outlined the advances in this active field and summarized useful strategies to improve the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance and separation capacities of MOFs.79, 192-198 In the following section, we provide a summary of the advancements achieved in recent years in this arena.
The SIFSIX MOFs have recently been demonstrated to be very promising physical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbents. In 2013, Nugent et al. fabricated three SIFSIX MOFs (SIFSIX-2-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, and SIFSIX-3-Zn) and studied their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture abilities. 165 Although SIFSIX-2-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, and SIFSIX-3-Zn all possess periodically arrayed functional $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ moieties (Figure 16), their pore sizes are different, which are $13.05\ \times\ 13.05\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2},\ 5.15\ \times\ 5.15\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ , and $3.84~\times~3.84~\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ , respectively. Gas sorption studies indicate that SIFSIX-3-Zn has the best $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture properties among the three MOFs, with not only a high volumetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of 84 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.1 bar but also very high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (post-combustion separation), $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (natural gas upgrading) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (pre-combustion separation) selectivities. Such adsorption performances are ascribed to a combined result of judiciously tailored pore size and functional fluorine moieties that significantly improve the interaction strength between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and SIFSIX-3-Zn, and was further validated by breakthrough experiments. Most importantly, these SIFSIX MOFs well maintain their selective adsorption $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\Nu_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , even in the presence of moisture, which is very crucial in practical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture scenarios. Subsequent endeavors have also been made by altering the metals species and/or anion pillars to further contract the pore size for better performance.199-200 The resulting NbOFFIVE-1-Ni displays the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake $(1.3~\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ and $51.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $(\mathrm{\mathop{STP}})/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and $400\mathrm{ppm}$ ) as a physical adsorbent.
In 2017, Liang et al. constructed a new Cu-based MOF, FJI-H14, from 2,5-di(1H-1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B T T A)}$ .166 FJI-H14 has a high density of open metal sites $\langle3.07\mathrm{\mol/L}\rangle$ and Lewis basic sites (LBS, $6.15\ \mathrm{mol/L}$ ), which is anticipated to have a high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake. Consequently, FJI-H14 displays an extraordinarily high volumetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of $171\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity as high as 51 for a $15/85\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixture. Based on simulation studies, the preferential $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding sites within FJI-H14 were further demonstrated to be open $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ sites and O atoms of carboxyl groups that function as Lewis bases. They can have strong Cu-O and C-O interactions with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules that give rise to high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity. Additionally, FJI-H14 is highly resistant to water and acid/base environments, making it a very promising material as a practical adsorbent for post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.
In 2015, Zhao et al. developed a strategy named pore space partition by regularly inserting symmetry-matching ligands into large pores to split them into smaller ones for enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes.201 They introduced the $C3$ -symmetric 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine (TPT) ligand into large pores of the MIL-88-type structures. The obtained the CPM-33 series show apparently superior $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity in gas sorption studies. Among them, CPM-33b in particular, has a gravimetric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $126~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, which is among the highest in MOFs without open metal sites. In 2016, Zhai et al. applied this strategy to construct a series of homo- or hetero- metallic MIL-88-type MOFs, and that found that $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\mathbf{V}$ -DHBDC $\mathrm{(DHBDC^{2-}}~=~2,5$ -dihydroxy-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) can achieve a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption amount as high as $151.6\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm with good $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity.171 It should be noted that the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity is accomplished with isosteric heat of adsorption as low as $20\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ , which would make the regeneration of the adsorbent consume less energy.
In 2016, Chen et al. realized the molecular sieving of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ through a MOF [Cu(quinoline-5-carboxylate) $\vert_{2}\vert_{\mathrm{n}}$ named Qc-5-Cu-sql (sql $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ square lattice) with fine-tuned pore-sizes.172 Qc-5-Cu-sql undergoes a phase transformation upon desolvation. The solvated $(\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{c}-\mathsf{J}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{s}\mathbf{q}\mathbf{l}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{a})$ and desolvated $(\mathrm{Qc}{-}5{-}\mathrm{Cu-sql-}\upbeta)$ phase have pore size of around 3.8 and $3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively. Since the kinetic diameters of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are 3.3, 3.64, and $3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively, the narrower pore in Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\upbeta$ is anticipated to exhibit a molecular sieving effect towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . As expected, Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\upbeta$ only adsorbs $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with a moderate uptake of $48.4~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ but completely block ${\bf N}_{2}$ $(0.3\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\left(1.3\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ , thus achieving a remarkably high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (40000) and $\mathrm{CO_{2}}/\mathrm{CH_{4}}\mathrm{~}(3300)$ selectivities at ambient conditions. Moreover, Qc-5-Cu-sql is stable in water vapor. Its separation ability was further validated by breakthrough experiments performed in dry and wet $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas mixtures.
Grafting alkylamine groups onto the pore surface of MOFs has been demonstrated to be an effective strategy to achieve highly selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ or $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at low pressure.169, 202-205 Particularly, in 2015 McDonald et al. reported the observation of unusual step-shaped $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms in $\mathbf{\xi}_{N,N},$ -dimethylethylenediamine (mmen) functionalized $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}$ , Mn, Fe, Co, Zn; dobpdc $^{4-}=4\mathcal{A}^{2}$ -dioxidobiphenyl- $_{\cdot3,3},$ -dicarboxylate), which is indicative of ―phase-change‖ during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.168 Spectroscopic, diffraction, and computational studies suggested that the sharp adsorption step was associated with an unprecedented cooperative process in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules insert into metal-amine bonds at a metal- and temperature- dependent pressure, triggering a reorganization of the amines into well-ordered chains of ammonium carbamate. Consequently, large $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation capacities can be achieved with small temperature swings and concomitant low regeneration energies. Then, an ultrahigh concentration of amine functional sites has been achieved in $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})(\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4})_{1.8}]$ by Liao et al. using hydrazine as the shortest diamine in amine grafting for low-concentration $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.169 Rely on the well-established carbamic acid formation, this new material has set a series of new records for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture (Table 4), as demonstrated by gas sorption isotherms, NMR spectra, cycling sorption kinetic and breakthrough experiments.
Another state-of-the-art strategy for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture is represented by biomimicking of carbonic anhydrase, which is an important and ubiquitous metalloenzyme that catalyzes the hydrolysis of carbon dioxide into bicarbonate species. The key structural fearure is the monodentate hydroxide at the metal nodes, which affords strong and reversible $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding. In 2015, Liao et al. firstly established the proof‑ of‑ concept of enzyme-like active site in MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, which is achieved in oxidized $\mathrm{[M_{2}C l_{2}(b b t a)]}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{ta}=1H,5H\cdot$ -benzo(1,2-d:4,5- $d^{\prime}$ )bistriazole; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , MAF-X25; $\textbf{M}=\textrm{C o}$ , MAF-X27).170 The post-synthetic immobilized $_\mathrm{M-OH}$ sites afford chemisorption mechanism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, resulting in very high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption affinity and uptake capacity. These materials are also applicable under humidity condition as demonstrated by breakthrough studies. In 2018, the Wade group and Dincӑ group separately demonstrated a more specific biomimetic MOF model of carbonic anhydrase for trace $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, featuring terminal $\mathrm{N}_{3}\mathrm{Zn-OH}$ units as the only active sites.206-207 These resultant materials show remarkable $\ C\overrightarrow{\mathbf{O}_{2}})$ capture performance in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture of $400\ \mathrm{ppm}$ concentration. These demonstrations of biomimetic behavior in MOF media afford an ideal platform at new dimension to understand the enzyme acivity and develop novel enzyme-inspired materials.
Simultaneously, in 2017, Lu et al. reported a redox-active MOF [MFM- ${.300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})]}$ which can be oxidized to its isostructural $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}}).^{208}$ The bridging hydroxyl group $\mathrm{\DeltaV^{III}-(O H){-}V^{I I I}}$ in $\mathbf{MFM-300(V^{III})}$ was deprotonated to $\mathrm{V^{IV}-O-V^{I V}}$ in $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ . $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{III}})$ achieved a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption amount of $6.0\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar based on gas adsorption analysis, while MFM-300(VIV) only has $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $3.54~\mathrm{mmol/g}$ . Such pronounced difference in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacities mainly derives from the different binding sites and subsequent packing modes of adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules in the two MOFs, which was revealed by neutron diffraction and inelastic scattering data. Specifically, $\mathbf{MFM-300(V^{III})}$ forms hydrogen bonds with the guest via the OH group and the O-donor of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in an end-on manner. By contrast, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -loaded $\mathrm{MFM}{-}300(\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{IV}})$ exhibits $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ bound side-on to the oxy group and sandwiched between two phenyl groups. This work well illustrates the conducive role the protons in hydroxyl groups in CO2 adsorption in MOFs.
## 3.9 Toxic gas removal
The capture and separation of toxic gases with MOFs have attracted increasing attention in recent years. Commonly encountered toxic gases include sulfur dioxide $(\mathsf{S O}_{2})$ , hydrogen sulfide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}\right)$ , chlorine $(\mathbf{Cl}_{2})$ , nitric oxide (NO), nitrogen dioxide $(\mathsf{N O}_{2})$ , and ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)$ . Most of these gases are released as waste gases from industrial processes, which incur severe environment issues and cause great harm to human health. Some thematic reviews have summarized the advances in this field.209-214 In this section, we will highlight several MOFs showing good potential to effectively remove these toxic gases.
In 2012, Yang et al. reported that NOTT-300 containing no amine groups can selectively bind $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ with the hydroxyl groups in pores.215 Several characterization methods, including in situ powder X-ray diffraction, inelastic neutron scattering studies and modelling, indicate that the hydroxyl groups bind $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ via the formation of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O=S=O(\delta-)\cdotsH(\delta+)-O}$ hydrogen bonds. Such hydrogen bonds are reinforced by the weak supramolecular interactions with C-H atoms on the aromatic rings of the framework. Since the $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ binding strength is not as strong as ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{N}}(\delta^{-}){\cdots}{\mathrm{C}}(\delta^{+}){\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ in carbamates (that result from the interaction between amine groups and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ), NOTT-300 has the potential to be utilized as an ―easy-on/easy-off‖ $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ capture systems, which carry fewer economic and environmental penalties.
$\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ capture can also be achieved in SIFSIX MOFs. In 2017, Cui et al. investigated the $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ capture ability of SIFSIX-1-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, SIFSIX-3-Zn, and SIFSIX-3-Ni.216 SIFSIX-1-Cu and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i display higher $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ uptake than those of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ at ambient conditions. Especially the $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ uptake of SIFSIX-1-Cu $\mathrm{(11.01~mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar) is remarkably high, and ultrahigh low-pressure $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ uptakes $\mathrm{(4.16~mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 0.01 bar; $2.31\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.002 bar) can be achieved in SIFSIX-2-Cu-i. The $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity on SIFSIX-2-Cu-i was calculated to be 86-89. While $\mathsf{S O}_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivities on SIFSIX-2-Cu-i and SIFSIX-1-Cu were 1285-3103 and 2510-3145, respectively. These excellent $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ separation performances result from strong host-guest interactions through $\mathbf{S}^{\delta^{+}\cdot\ldots\mathrm{F}^{\delta^{-}}}$ electrostatic interaction and multiple $\mathrm{O^{\delta_{\tilde{\cdot}\cdot\cdot\tilde{H}^{\delta+}}}}$ dipole-dipole interactions, as elucidated by first principles density functional theory calculations and powder $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray diffraction experiments. Breakthrough experiments further validate their ability to remove trace $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ .
The capture and separation of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is found to be associated with the open metal sites within MOFs. In 2016 Rieth et al. reported the high and reversible $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake in mesoporous triazolate MOFs $\begin{array}{l l}{\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}}&{\mathrm{(M)}}\end{array}=\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{Mn}$ , Co, Ni; BTDD $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ bis(1H-1,2,3-triazole[4,5-b],[4’,5’-i])dibenzo[1,4]dioxin).217 The same group in 2018 further studied a contracted series of triazolate MOFs, that is, microporous $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BBTA}$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{M}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{0}}$ , Ni, Cu; BBTA $\risingdotseq1H,\rightharpoonup H$ -benzo(1,2-d),(4,5-d’)bistriazole) for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capture and separation.218 Both $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BB}\mathbf{TA}$ and $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BTDD}$ feature one dimensional hexagonal pores with regularly spaced open metal sites. The smaller pore diameter of $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BBTA}$ $(1.3~\mathrm{{nm})}$ than that of $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BTDD}$ $(2.3~\mathrm{nm})$ leads to the higher density of open metal sites in $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BBTA}$ , which is beneficial for higher $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacities. Under equilibrium conditions at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ displayed a record static $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacity of $19.79\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ , which is more than twice the industry standard. In dynamic breakthrough experiments, dynamic $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacity of Co2Cl2BBTA can reach $8.56\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ . They attributed the higher $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacity in smaller-pore $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{T}\mathbf{A}$ not only to the increase in open metal site density but also to the cooperative proximity effects that enhance the hydrogen bonding interactions between adsorbed $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules. In terms of stability upon $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption, the trends are in line with the water substitution rates in simple metal-aquo complexes, which can act as an easy guide for designing future $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capture adsorbents.
In 2018, Han et al. reported a reversible and selective adsorption of $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ in a robust MOF, MFM-300(Al).148 MFM-300(Al) shows a $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ uptake as high as 14.1 $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. Moreover, the $\mathrm{NO}_{2}/\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\Nu{\cal O}_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ IAST selectivities at these conditions were calculated to be 18.1, 248, and $>10000$ , respectively. Based on the results from various characterization methods, they found that there exists five different types of soft supramolecular interaction that cooperatively bind both $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O}_{4}$ molecules within MFM-300(Al), which take the main responsibility for the highly selective adsorption of $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ over other gas species. The captured $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O}_{4}$ afford one-dimensional helical $(\mathrm{NO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{\infty}$ chains that are stabilized by multiple weak intermolecular dipole interactions. Breakthrough experiments with several diluted $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ streams $\mathrm{5000~ppm}$ diluted in $\mathrm{He}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , 4000 ppm in $\mathrm{He}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , or 1666 ppm in ${\mathrm{He}}/{\mathrm{SO}}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) further confirmed that MFM-300(Al) is capable to efficiently separate the $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ in low concentration from these gas mixtures.
Ιn 2010, Shimomura et al. reported the selective absorption of NO by a structurally and electronically dynamic MOF [Zn(TCNQ-TCNQ)bpy] $\mathrm{(TCNQ~=~}$ tetracyanoquinodimethane; bpy $\mathit{\Pi}=\mathit{\Pi}4,4^{\bullet}$ -bipyridyl). This unprecedented preference arises from the concerted effect of the charge-transfer interaction between TCNQ and NO molecules as well as the switchable gate opening and closing of the pores in the framework.219
In 2015, Bloch et al. reported the strong binding of NO at 298 K in Fe2(dobdc) featuring redox-active open metal sites.168 $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}$ (dobdc) displays an extraordinarily steep step at the low-pressure region in the NO adsorption isotherm, with adsorption capacity high higher than $16\mathrm{\mt}\%$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.2 bar (corresponding to one NO molecule per open Fe site). The fact that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ approaches its saturation capacity (corresponding to $0.95\mathrm{NO}$ molecules per Fe site) near 0.007 bar indicates the strong binding of NO molecule in the framework. Characterized by infrared, UV-Vis, and Mössbauer spectroscopies, neutron powder diffraction, and magnetic susceptibility studies, the strong binding mainly results from the electron transfer from the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites to form $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ NO- adducts. Furthermore, it was found that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathsf{N O})_{2}$ (dobdc) can release bound NO steadily under humid conditions over the course of more than 10 days, suggesting its potential to be applied for NO storage and release, such as in certain biomedical applications.
MOFs have also been investigated to capture fluorocarbons and chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs). In this aspect, Chen et al. in 2015 fabricated a mesoporous fluorinated MOFs, MOFF-5, from an extensively fluorinated tritopic tetrazolate-based ligand.220 MOFF-5 has an accessible surface area of $2445\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , which translates into remarkably high adsorption capacities of fluorocarbons and CFCs. Their weight capacities can be up to $225\%$ . Similarly, Lin et al. in 2015 studied the adsorption of a typical fluorocarbon R22 $\mathrm{\CHCl}\mathrm{F}_{2})$ in a new series of isoreticular constructed from $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyrazolate and different linear dicarboxylate ligands.221 It was found that the pore size and/ or porosity of the MOFs can affect the R22 adsorption performance, with a highest achieved uptake of $0.73{\scriptstyle-0.97}\mathrm{g/g}(0.62{\scriptstyle-0.65}\mathrm{g/cm})$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.
In 2018, Xie et al. constructed two Zr-MOFs named BUT-66 and BUT-67 from two long and angular lignds $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(benzene-1,3-diyl)dibenzoate $(\mathbf{BDB}^{2-})$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(naphthalene-2,7-diyl)dibenzoate $(\mathrm{NDB}^{2-})$ , respectively.222 BUT-66 and BUT-67 possess two-fold interpenetrated pcu network structures and feature small hydrophobic pores that are suitable to capture aromatic volatile organic compounds (VOCs) from humid air (Figure 17). BUT-66, in particular, displayed apparently higher benzene adsorption capacity $(1.75\mathrm{\mmol/cm}^{3})$ ) at high temperature and low pressure than some benchmark materials, like MCM-41, MIL-101(Cr), ZIF-8, PAF-1, and Carboxen 1000. Additionally, BUT-66 is also capable to capture trace benzene (several parts per million) in humid air (relative humidity $50\%$ ) wth a high capacity of ca. $0.28\mathrm{\mmol/cm}^{3}$ . Single-crystal analyses of BUT-66 loaded with guest molecules suggest that its high performance originates from combined result of the small hydrophobic pores, mutually less interference between hydrophobic and hydrophilic adsorption sites, and the local rotatable phenyl rings within the rigid overall framework. This work demonstrates the promise of MOFs as advanced materials for practical application in air cleaning.
Carbon monoxide (CO) is an industrially important gas, either as valuable carbon products or as catalyst poisoning impurities during important chemical production. There are various needs to enrich/removal CO from the gaseous products before any further applications. The molecular size and properties of CO make it very challenging to separate from other small gas molecules such as ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . But by virtue of some unique structural features of MOFs, relevant separations can be still realized. For example, Sato et al. reported a soft nanoporous MOF $[\mathrm{Cu(aip)(H_{2}O)}]$ $(\arcsin=5$ -azidoisophthalate) for $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation.223 This MOF can undergo a global structural transformation during the binding interaction of CO with open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites, which collaboratively enables the implementation of selective CO capture from $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ . Similar CO sorption behavior was also observed in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (bbta) $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{ta}}&{{}=}\end{array}$
1H,5H-benzo(1,2-d:4,5-d’)bistriazole) by Reed et al.224 This MOF features coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ sites, which can undergo a cooperative spin transition during CO adsorption, resulting in stepwise CO sorption with large separation capacity and selectivity over ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
There are also needs for purification of CO during emerging chemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. In this context, the removal of easily polarized $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from the mixture would be much more directly realized. Chen et al. demonstrated such purification can be readily achieved by using $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -selective MOFs as adsorbents,225 referring to SIFSIX-3-Ni and TIFSIX-2-Cu-i, which showing remarkable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}$ selectivity. Thus high-purity CO can be readily obtained as demonstrated by breakthrough experiments.
## 3.10 Some other gas/vapor separations
Xenon/krypton $(\mathrm{Xe/Kr})$ separation is of great industrial significance, affording important noble gases for applications in lighting, electronics, and the medical industry. Current industrial Xe production relies on cryogenic distillation of a by-product $\mathrm{Xe/Kr}$ mixture during air liquefaction, which is high energy-intensive and capital-intensive. In recent years, MOFs-based adsorptive separation technology shows great potential in energy-effective separation of Xe/Kr. There are more than 20 MOFs that have been carefully investigated for Xe/Kr separation. Comprehensive evaluation of these candidates has been performed by Banerjee and Thallapally et al.226 Here we aim to briefly introduce this application by presenting selected examples.
The proof-of-concept of MOFs for Xe/Kr separation was established by Mueller et al. in 2006, as demonstrated by pressure–swing adsorption process on HKUST-1 in $6/94\mathrm{Xe/Kr}$ mixture at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 40 bar.72 The breakthrough results shows that the Xe can be capture from the mixture to a level of $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Great progresses have been achieved under continuous efforts on this separation since 2012.226 For example, in 2016, Banerjee et al. identified and affirmed a calcium $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ –sulfonyldibenzoate MOF SBMOF-1 as one of the most selective adsorbent for $\mathrm{Xe/Kr}$ separation after a high-throughput computational screening of large MOF database.227 The microporous SBMOF-1 shows a pore size of about $4.2\textup{\AA}$ , and well accommodation for Xe atoms with diameter of about 4.0 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ as revealed by crystallographic results of Xe-loaded SBMOF-1, affording a very high adsorption affinity of ${\sim}35\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ . Hence, SBMOF-1 shows remarkable capture capacity and $\mathrm{Xe/Kr}$ selectivity $(\sim16)$ for trace Xe removal. Breakthrough analysis demonstrated the highest capture capacity of $13.2\ \mathrm{mmol/kg}$ for trace Xe from waste gas simulant processing. Further incorporation with functional sites into ultramicroporous MOFs can enhance the separation performance, as demonstrated by CROFOUR-1/2-Ni reported in 2016.228 The optimal pore size and $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ moiety enable CROFOUR-1-Ni to show a highest $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ of $37.4~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at zero loading and a $\mathrm{Xe/Kr}$ selectivity of 22.
The inert nature of noble gases appears to limit boosting of the binding affinity of MOFs for these gases, while the Kr-selective sorbents afford another approach for efficient Xe separation from Kr. Fernandez et al. reported a flexible MOF (FMOFCu, also [Cu(hfipbb)(H2hfipbb)0.5]) as a rare example for inverse Kr/Xe separation.229 This MOF is described to exhibit temperature-dependent gating effect, referring to the shrinking of pore aperture size and simultaneous decreasing flexibility at lower temperature, which would restrict the diffusion of the larger Xe atoms $(4.0\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and thus separate $\mathrm{Xe/Kr}$ mixture under kinetic nonequilibrium status. Accordingly, FMOFCu shows decreasing Xe uptake upon temperature drop below $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , and simultaneously keep continuous increasing uptake for Kr, resulting in inverse $\mathrm{Kr/Xe}$ separation. This inverse sorption behavior disappears at temperature above $273\mathrm{K}$ .
Purification of the $\mathrm{C}_{8}$ alkylaromatics $o$ -xylene (OX), m-xylene (MX), $p$ -xylene (PX), and ethylbenzene (EB) is perceived as a very crucial task. However, the separation remains very challenging owing to the similar shapes, polarities and boiling points of the molecules. At relatively high ambient temperatures, $\mathrm{C}_{8}$ alkylaromatics are in the form of gas vapor, which makes adsorption-based separation of $\mathrm{C}_{8}$ isomers feasible. As a type of highly porous materials, MOFs are regarded as favorable adsorbents to realize the separation of xylene isomers in gas phase. Some progress has been made in recent years, which have been well summarized.15-16 Here we aim to update this application by selected examples.
In 2018, Gonzalez et al. investigated two MOFs, Co2(dobdc) and Co2(m-dobdc), for the separation of xylene isomers.230 Based on the single component isotherms, $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ is found to have different binding affinities of these $\mathrm{C}_{8}$ alkylaromatics molecules, which follows the order of $\mathrm{OX\^{>}\ E B>\ M X>\ P X}$ . Multicomponent vapor-phase breakthrough experiments show that $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ is able to distinguish these species. Single crystal X-ray diffraction measurement reveal that separation is mainly attributed to the nuanced differences in the interactions of OX, EB, and MX with two adjacent $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites and the inability of PX to interact with a second $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ center.
Deuterium is widely used in a number of commercial and scientific applications, especially shows great potential as an important energy source. Owing to its extremely low natural abundance of $0.0156\%$ , the industrial deuterium production is currently based on enrichment from isotope mixtures through chemical exchange method (Girdler sulfide process) and cryogenic distillation (at $24\mathrm{~K~}$ ), which are complicate and energy-intensive. In contrast, kinetic adsorptive separation based on kinetic quantum molecular sieving mechanism in porous materials at elevated temperatures (e.g. 77 K) is proposed to be more energy-efficient, which originates from different diffusion barriers related to the difference in the zero-point energy of the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ isotopes. Accordingly, adsorbents with ultramicroporous structure showing a size difference of the de Broglie wavelength with the gas diameter are more favorable.82 The first experimental example of MOFs for quantum sieving of $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is demonstrated by Chen et al. in 2008 using a metalloligand MOF (M’MOF-1) that integrates narrow confined pore structure with open metal sites.140 The pore size of M’MOF-1 is about $5.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Although gas sorption study reveals that M’MOF-1 shows slightly higher adsorption enthalpy for ${\bf D}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , the ${\bf D}_{2}$ still shows a significantly faster adsorption kinetic than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , resulting in potential $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation. This is because the heavier ${\bf D}_{2}$ shows lower diffusion activation energy than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ which originate from its shorter de Broglie wavelength in confined pore channels at cryogenic temperature. In 2013, Teufel et al. reported abnormal hydrogen sorption phenomena in an ultramicroporous MOF $[\mathrm{Zn}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}(\mathrm{BBTA})_{3}]$ (MFU-4) featuring temperature-triggered gate opening for $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation.231 The experimental $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity under non-equilibrium condition was firstly realized in this work by using a thermal-desorption spectroscopy apparatus. Owing to extremely confined pore aperture, this MOF shows a non-equilibrium $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity of 7.5 at 10 mbar and $60\mathrm{K}$ upon gas mixture exposure for $15\mathrm{min}$ before achieving equilibrium.
MOFs with open metal sites for enhanced hydrogen binding affinity have also been evaluated for selective quantum sieving of ${\bf D}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . For example, Fitz-Gerald et al. demonstrated that Ni-MOF-74 shows a largest adsorption heat difference of 1.4 $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ for $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , resulting in a selectivity of 5 at low pressure and $77~\mathsf{K}._{\cdot}^{232}$ In a recent work, Weinrauch et al. revealed that $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ -MFU-4l with open $\mathsf{\overline{{C u}}(I)}$ sites shows remarkable adsorption enthalpy for ${\bf D}_{2}$ $\left(35\mathrm{\kJ/mol}\right)$ with a difference of 2.5 $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ,233 which enables this MOF to capture D2 with an experimental selectivity of 11.1 at $100\mathrm{K}$ for 1:1 $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ mixture.
## 4. MOF-based membranes for gas separation
To explore a highly efficient way to separate gas mixtures, membrane separation stands out as a promising strategy. Increasing research attention has been attracted worldwide to developing novel MOF-based membranes to accomplish the challenging separation tasks in an energy-economic manner. The separation performance of membranes is evaluated by two criteria, namely, permeability and selectivity. Some reviews have summarized the fabrication of MOF-based membranes234-235, and the advances in their separation performance over the years.236-240 In this section, we will highlight some significant progress made in recent years in this active research field.
In 2014, Peng et al. reported the preparation of nanosheets from a layered MOF $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bim})_{4}$ $(\mathrm{bim\bar{\mathbf{\theta}}=\Phi}$ benzimidazolate) with maintenance of its morphological and structural integrity.241 The exfoliation technique they employed was a soft-physical process in which the pristine layered MOF was wet-ball milled and exfoliated in volatile solvent subsequently. The resulting nanosheets thus possesses large lateral areas and high crystallinities, and were utilized as the building blocks for ultrathin molecular sieving membranes. They intentionally suppressed the lamellar stacking of nanosheets by a hot-drop coating process to minimize the blockage of gas molecule passways in restacking. Since the aperture size of a single-layered $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bim})_{4}$ nanosheet is around $2.1\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ whose effective pore size might be larger due to structural flexibility, the generated membrane achieved an effective separation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}(2.9\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}(3.3\mathring\mathrm{A})$ through molecular sieving (Figure 18). By optimizing the ${\bf{a}}{\mathrm{-}}{\bf{A l}}_{2}{\bf{O}}_{3}$ disk temperature to maximize the disordered stacking between $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bim})_{4}$ nanosheet for higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance, the molecular sieving membrane eventually achieved a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeance (2700 GPU) and a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity (291) simultaneously, which surpasses the Robeson’s upper-bound for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas pair.
Similarly, a membrane based on pure MOF nanosheets was also realized by Wang et al. in 2017. They fabricated nm-thick molecular sieving membranes derived from porous two-dimensional MAMS-1 nanosheets.242 These membranes possess pore openings parallel to gas concentration gradient to allow high gas permeation flux and high selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation. Furthermore, the membrane shows a reversed thermo-switchable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ permeation, which originate from the molecular flexibility of the building metal-organic nanosheets.
To guarantee the mechanical stability of the membranes, most MOF-based membranes are produced in the form of mixed matrix membrane as composite materials. Typically, the matrix is a type of polymer, such as 6FDA-DAM. In 2010, Bae et al. reported introducing ZIF-90 crystals with submicrometer into 6FDA-DAM polymer to fabricate ZIF-90/6FDA-DAM membranes with high performance for CO2/CH4 separation.243
In 2014, Rodenas et al. employed a bottom-up strategy to synthesize CuBDC MOF nanosheets with lamellae of micrometer lateral dimensions. By incorporating MOF nanosheets into polymer matrices, they fabricated a mixed matrix membrane possessing excellent ability to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gas mixtures with an unusual increase in selectivity with pressure.244 Compared with isotropic crystals, the superior occupation of the membrane cross-section by the MOF nanosheets endows the membrane with better molecular discriminating ability and eliminates unselective permeation pathways.
In 2016, Bachman et al. prepared some composite materials by dispersing $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}$ (dobdc) MOF nanocrystals within 6FDA-DAM polyimide. These membranes display enhanced $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity and greater ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{4}$ permeability and improved membrane stability than pure polymer membrane. Additionally, the framework-polymer interactions were found to reduce the chain mobility of the polymer and boost the separation performance of the membrane as well.245
In 2018, Liu et al. reported incorporation of submicrometer-sized RE-fcu-MOF crystals into 6FDA-DAM polymer matrix to generate hybrid membrane materials.246 The hybrid membrane showed significantly enhanced performance in removing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and separating butane isomers $(\mathrm{nC_{4}}$ and iC4). The property originates from precise control of the linker from 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylate (naph) to fumarate (fum) to give rise to contracted MOF pore apertures and delimit the size of the triangular window (Figure 19). As the sole entrance to the fcu-MOF pore, these triangular windows discriminate gas molecules and achieve separation purposes. This rational-design hybrid approach offers a toolbox for improving the transport properties of membranes containing hybridized MOF fillers with optimal apertures as molecular sieves. Notably, the Y-fum-fcu-MOF/6FDA-DAM membrane was also reported to exhibit good CO2/CH4 separation performance.247
